window.BOOK_SECTIONS = [{"id": "overview", "part": "Front Matter", "label": "0. Overview & Notation", "verified": true, "markdown": "# A Lie-Interpretation of Robotics and Control\n\nVersion-controlled textbook, executable Google Colab tutorials, and reusable engineering skills developed under the **Elephant–Goldfish** architecture:\n- **Goldfish Agents:** Develop each chapter, Colab notebook (`.ipynb`), and reusable skill (`SKILL.md`) independently.\n- **Elephant Orchestrator:** Enforces the Master Notation & Consistency Standard, verifies equations and code against primary literature, and integrates verified assets into the master manuscript and Google Drive (`Gemini Spark`).\n\n## Directory Structure\n- `chapters/` — Verified Markdown chapters (`ch01_*.md` through `ch16_*.md`)\n- `notebooks/` — Executable Google Colab tutorials (`ch01_*.ipynb` through `ch16_*.ipynb`)\n- `skills/` — Reusable `SKILL.md` workflow guides (`chXX-*/SKILL.md`)\n- `NOTATION_STANDARD.md` — Non-negotiable cross-chapter symbol, equation, figure, and table standard\n- `TOC.md` — Master 16-chapter architecture (Parts I–VII)\n\n\n---\n\n# Master Notation, Terminology & Consistency Standard\n\n1. **Equations:** `(C.N)` where `C` is chapter number, `N` is equation number (e.g., `Eq. (2.4)`).\n2. **Figures & Tables:** `Fig. C.N`, `Table C.N`.\n3. **Formal Statements:** `Def. C.N`, `Thm. C.N`, `Prop. C.N`, `Lemma C.N`, `Assumption C.N`, `Remark C.N`.\n4. **Hat & Vee Maps:** `(·)∧ : ℝ³ → so(3)` such that `x∧ y = x × y`; `(·)∨ : so(3) → ℝ³`.\n5. **Angular Velocities:** `Ω ∈ ℝ³` for `SO(3)` (`Ṙ = R Ω∧`); `ω ∈ ℝ³` for `S²` (`q̇ = ω × q`, `ω · q = 0`).\n6. **Errors & Transport Maps:**\n   - `SO(3)`: `Ψ_R = ½ Tr(I₃ − R_dᵀ R)`, `e_R = ½ (R_dᵀ R − Rᵀ R_d)∨`, `T(R, R_d) Ω_d = Rᵀ R_d Ω_d`, `e_Ω = Ω − Rᵀ R_d Ω_d`.\n   - `S²`: `Ψ_q = 1 − q · q_d`, `e_q = (q_d∧)² q`, `T(q, q_d) q̇_d = (q_d × q̇_d) × q`, `e_q̇ = q̇ − (q_d × q̇_d) × q`, `e_ω = ω − (−q∧²) ω_d`.\n7. **Infinitesimal Variations:** `δR = R_d η∧` (`η ∈ ℝ³`), `δΩ = Ω_d∧ η + η̇`; `δq = ξ × q_d` (`ξ · q_d = 0`), `(ξ × q_d) · ω_d + q_d · δω = 0`.\n8. **Symmetry & Local Connection:** `ξ_body = A(r) ṙ` on principal fiber bundle `Q = G × B`.\n\n\n---\n\n# Master Textbook Architecture (Parts I–VII)\n\n- **Part I: Foundations — Smooth Manifolds & Matrix Lie Groups**\n  - Ch. 1: Why Coordinate-Free Geometry? Singularities, Unwinding & Global Topology\n  - Ch. 2: Core Manifolds (`S¹/SO(2)`, `S²`, `SO(3)`, `S³`, `SE(3)`) & Product Spaces\n  - Ch. 3: Variations, Configuration Errors & Transport Maps\n- **Part II: Modeling & Symbolic Derivation**\n  - Ch. 4: Global Lagrangian, Newton-Euler & Lagrange-d'Alembert Mechanics on Manifolds\n  - Ch. 5: Symbolic Derivation with `PyGeomech` & Modular Multi-Body Formulations\n- **Part III: Geometric Control & Stability Proofs**\n  - Ch. 6: Geometric Tracking Control & Almost-Global Lyapunov Stability\n  - Ch. 7: Variation-Based Linearization, State-Constrained Controllability & LTV-LQR\n  - Ch. 8: Robust, Adaptive (`L₁`) & Safety-Critical (`CLF-CBF-QP`) Geometric Control\n- **Part IV: Complexity Reduction via Symmetry, Flatness & Zero Dynamics**\n  - Ch. 9: Symmetries, Principal Fiber Bundles & Local Connections (Choset's Framework)\n  - Ch. 10: Differential Flatness & Differentially-Flat Hybrid Systems\n  - Ch. 11: Hybrid Zero Dynamics (HZD) & Virtual Constraints on Manifolds (`SO(2)`/`SO(3)`)\n- **Part V: Trajectory Optimization & Variational Integrators**\n  - Ch. 12: Lie Group Variational Integrators (LGVI) & Direct Collocation\n  - Ch. 13: Error-State MPC & On-Manifold Interior Point Methods (`RIPM`, `LieIPM`)\n- **Part VI: State Estimation & Physical AI on Manifolds**\n  - Ch. 14: Filtering on Lie Groups (`InEKF`, `S²`-EKF, `UKF-M`)\n  - Ch. 15: Symmetry-Equivariant RL, Residual Geometric Control (`ResGAC`), & Continuous Neural Embeddings (`CHyLL`)\n- **Part VII: Integrated Robotic Case Studies**\n  - Ch. 16: Aerial Manipulation, Legged Locomotion, Limbless Robots & Whole-Body Humanoids\n\n\n```mermaid\nflowchart LR\n  A[Configuration Manifold M] --> B[Coordinate-Free Dynamics]\n  B --> C[Symmetry & Flatness Reduction]\n  C --> D[Geometric Control & LieIPM]\n  D --> E[InEKF & Physical AI]\n```\n"}, {"id": "ch01", "part": "Part I: Foundations", "label": "Ch 1. Why Coordinate-Free?", "verified": true, "chapterFile": "ch01_why_coordinate_free.md", "notebook": "ch01_singularities_unwinding.ipynb", "skill": "ch01-manifold-diagnostics", "diagramKey": "s3_double_cover", "markdown": "﻿Chapter 1: Why Coordinate-Free Geometry? Singularities, Unwinding & Global Topology\nCore Thesis: Control laws designed in local coordinates or on covering spaces inherit artificial singularities, break physical symmetries, and suffer from unwinding. By formulating kinematics, configuration errors, and feedback directly on the configuration manifold—$SO(3)$ for rigid-body attitude and $S^2$ for unit-vector directions—we obtain globally well-defined, singularity-free controllers with almost-global exponential stability.\n\n\n________________\n\n\nLearning Objectives\nBy the end of this chapter, you will be able to:\n\n\n1. Define the Special Orthogonal Group $SO(3)$, its Lie algebra $\\mathfrak{so}(3)$, and the hat/vee isomorphism $(\\cdot)^\\wedge, (\\cdot)^\\vee$.\n2. Derive the ZYX Euler-angle kinematic Jacobian $E(\\eta)$, prove rank deficiency at pitch $\\theta = \\pm \\frac{\\pi}{2}$ (gimbal lock), and explain why local-coordinate PD control loses feedback invariance.\n3. Explain the $S^3 \\to SO(3)$ double cover for unit quaternions and demonstrate how continuous quaternion feedback induces the unwinding phenomenon (and why naive discontinuous sign-switching induces chattering).\n4. State the topological obstruction to continuous global stabilization on compact manifolds without boundary (Bhat & Bernstein, 2000; Milnor) and interpret almost-global exponential stability (AGES) around measure-zero antipodal equilibria.\n5. Formulate and evaluate the coordinate-free configuration error functions $\\Psi_R(R, R_d)$ on $SO(3)$ and $\\Psi_q(q, q_d)$ on $S^2$.\n\n\n________________\n\n\n1.1 The Coordinate Trap: Why $\\mathbb{R}^n$ Intuition Fails on Curved Spaces\nClassical control theory is built on Euclidean vector spaces $\\mathbb{R}^n$, where states can be subtracted component-wise ($e = x - x_d$), a single global coordinate chart covers the entire space, and linearizing around an equilibrium yields a globally valid coordinate representation. Mechanical and robotic systems—quadrotors, satellites, cable-suspended loads, robotic manipulators, and legged robots—do not live in $\\mathbb{R}^n$. Their configurations evolve on smooth, curved manifolds such as the three-dimensional rotation group $SO(3)$ and the two-sphere $S^2$.\n\n\nAttempting to force a curved manifold into $\\mathbb{R}^n$ via local coordinates (such as Euler angles) or redundant covering spaces (such as unit quaternions) introduces pathologies that are mathematical artifacts of the representation, not physical properties of the robot.\n\n\nDef. 1.1 (Special Orthogonal Group $SO(3)$ and Lie Algebra $\\mathfrak{so}(3)$). The configuration space of rigid-body orientations in three-dimensional space is the Special Orthogonal Group: $$ SO(3) = \\left{ R \\in \\mathbb{R}^{3 \\times 3} ;\\middle|; R^\\top R = I_3, ; \\det(R) = +1 \\right}, \\tag{1.1} $$ where $I_3$ is the $3 \\times 3$ identity matrix. Its tangent space at the identity $I_3$ is the Lie algebra $\\mathfrak{so}(3) = { \\hat{\\Omega} \\in \\mathbb{R}^{3 \\times 3} \\mid \\hat{\\Omega}^\\top = -\\hat{\\Omega} }$, the vector space of $3 \\times 3$ real skew-symmetric matrices.\n\n\nDef. 1.2 (Hat and Vee Maps). The hat map $(\\cdot)^\\wedge : \\mathbb{R}^3 \\to \\mathfrak{so}(3)$ is the linear isomorphism defined such that for all $x, y \\in \\mathbb{R}^3$, $x^\\wedge y = x \\times y$: $$ x^\\wedge = \\begin{bmatrix} 0 & -x_3 & x_2 \\ x_3 & 0 & -x_1 \\ -x_2 & x_1 & 0 \\end{bmatrix} \\in \\mathfrak{so}(3), \\quad x = \\begin{bmatrix} x_1 \\ x_2 \\ x_3 \\end{bmatrix} \\in \\mathbb{R}^3. \\tag{1.2} $$ Its inverse, the vee map $(\\cdot)^\\vee : \\mathfrak{so}(3) \\to \\mathbb{R}^3$, extracts the unique vector $x$ from the skew-symmetric matrix $x^\\wedge$, i.e., $(x^\\wedge)^\\vee = x$.\n\n\nUsing Eq. (1.2), the rotational kinematics of a rigid body with body-frame angular velocity $\\Omega \\in \\mathbb{R}^3$ evolve on $SO(3)$ according to the left-invariant differential equation: $$ \\dot{R} = R \\Omega^\\wedge. \\tag{1.3} $$ Notice that Eq. (1.3) has no denominators, no trigonometric singularities, and preserves $R(t) \\in SO(3)$ for all $t \\ge 0$.\n\n\n________________\n\n\n1.2 Euler-Angle Singularities, Gimbal Lock, and Loss of Feedback Invariance\nTo avoid working with $3 \\times 3$ matrices subject to $6$ orthogonality constraints, engineers frequently parameterize $R \\in SO(3)$ using three minimal local coordinates, such as ZYX (yaw-pitch-roll) Euler angles $\\eta = (\\psi, \\theta, \\phi)^\\top \\in \\mathbb{R}^3$: $$ R(\\psi, \\theta, \\phi) = R_z(\\psi) R_y(\\theta) R_x(\\phi), \\tag{1.4} $$ where $\\psi$ is yaw, $\\theta$ is pitch, and $\\phi$ is roll. Differentiating Eq. (1.4) with respect to time and matching $\\dot{R} = R \\Omega^\\wedge$ from Eq. (1.3) yields the body angular velocity $\\Omega = E(\\theta, \\phi)\\dot{\\eta}$: $$ \\Omega = \\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix} -\\sin\\theta & 0 & 1 \\ \\cos\\theta \\sin\\phi & \\cos\\phi & 0 \\ \\cos\\theta \\cos\\phi & -\\sin\\phi & 0 \\end{bmatrix}}_{E(\\theta, \\phi)} \\begin{bmatrix} \\dot{\\psi} \\ \\dot{\\theta} \\ \\dot{\\phi} \\end{bmatrix}. \\tag{1.5} $$ Inverting $E(\\theta, \\phi)$ gives the Euler-angle kinematic differential equation $\\dot{\\eta} = E^{-1}(\\theta, \\phi)\\Omega$: $$ \\begin{bmatrix} \\dot{\\psi} \\ \\dot{\\theta} \\ \\dot{\\phi} \\end{bmatrix} = \\frac{1}{\\cos\\theta} \\begin{bmatrix} 0 & \\sin\\phi & \\cos\\phi \\ 0 & \\cos\\theta\\cos\\phi & -\\cos\\theta\\sin\\phi \\ \\cos\\theta & \\sin\\theta\\sin\\phi & \\sin\\theta\\cos\\phi \\end{bmatrix} \\Omega. \\tag{1.6} $$\n\n\nProp. 1.1 (Rank Deficiency and Loss of Feedback Invariance at Gimbal Lock). For the ZYX Euler-angle parameterization in Eq. (1.4)–(1.6):\n\n\n1. $\\det(E(\\theta, \\phi)) = -\\cos\\theta$, so $E(\\theta, \\phi)$ becomes singular ($\\operatorname{rank}(E) = 2 < 3$) whenever $\\theta = \\pm \\frac{\\pi}{2}$.\n2. At $\\theta = \\pm \\frac{\\pi}{2}$, the yaw and roll axes align in inertial space, causing $\\dot{\\psi}$ and $\\dot{\\phi}$ to become collinear and rendering $E^{-1}(\\theta, \\phi)$ undefined.\n3. A linear coordinate PD law $\\tau = -K_p(\\eta - \\eta_d) - K_d\\dot{\\eta}$ is not left-invariant on $SO(3)$: its closed-loop behavior depends on the arbitrary choice of inertial reference frame and blows up as $\\theta \\to \\pm \\frac{\\pi}{2}$.\n\n\nProof Sketch. Expanding $\\det(E)$ from Eq. (1.5) along the third column gives $\\det(E) = 1 \\cdot (\\cos\\theta\\sin\\phi(-\\sin\\phi) - \\cos\\theta\\cos\\phi(\\cos\\phi)) = -\\cos\\theta$. When $\\theta = +\\frac{\\pi}{2}$, the first and third columns of $E$ are $(-1, 0, 0)^\\top$ and $(1, 0, 0)^\\top$, which are linearly dependent. Consequently, the column space of $E$ collapses to $\\operatorname{span}{e_1, (0, \\cos\\phi, -\\sin\\phi)^\\top}$, a 2D subspace of $\\mathbb{R}^3$. Furthermore, for two rotations $R, R_d \\in SO(3)$ rotated by a common inertial offset $Q \\in SO(3)$ ($R \\mapsto QR, R_d \\mapsto QR_d$), the physical relative error $R_d^\\top R$ is invariant, whereas $\\eta(QR) - \\eta(QR_d) \\neq \\eta(R) - \\eta(R_d)$ in general, breaking feedback invariance. $\\blacksquare$\nWorked Example 1.1: Gimbal Lock Jacobian Collapse & Rate Blow-Up\nSuppose a quadrotor executes a smooth pitch-up maneuver with constant body angular velocity $\\Omega = (0, 0.5, 0.5)^\\top;\\text{rad/s}$ as its pitch approaches $\\theta = 89^\\circ$ ($\\epsilon = \\frac{\\pi}{2} - \\theta = 1^\\circ \\approx 0.01745;\\text{rad}$) with $\\phi = 45^\\circ$. From Eq. (1.6):\n\n\n* $\\cos\\theta = \\sin(1^\\circ) \\approx 0.01745$.\n* $\\dot{\\psi} = \\frac{1}{\\cos\\theta}(\\Omega_2 \\sin\\phi + \\Omega_3 \\cos\\phi) = \\frac{1}{0.01745}(0.5 \\cdot 0.7071 + 0.5 \\cdot 0.7071) \\approx 40.52;\\text{rad/s}$.\n* $\\dot{\\phi} \\approx \\tan\\theta (\\Omega_2 \\sin\\phi + \\Omega_3 \\cos\\phi) \\approx 40.51;\\text{rad/s}$.\n\n\nEven though the physical angular speed $|\\Omega| = \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\approx 0.707;\\text{rad/s}$ is completely benign, the Euler-angle rates explode past $40;\\text{rad/s}$ (over $2300^\\circ/\\text{s}$) and the Jacobian condition number $\\kappa(E) = \\sigma_{\\max}(E)/\\sigma_{\\min}(E)$ diverges as $O(1/\\cos\\theta)$. See Fig. 1.1 in the companion notebook ch01_singularities_unwinding.ipynb.\n\n\nRepresentation\n\tSpace\n\tDimension\n\tConstraints\n\tSingularities?\n\tUniqueness\n\tGlobal Topology\n\tEuler Angles $(\\psi, \\theta, \\phi)$\n\t$\\mathbb{R}^3$ (local)\n\t3\n\t0\n\tYes ($\\theta = \\pm \\frac{\\pi}{2}$)\n\tNon-unique ($2\\pi$ wrap + gimbal lock)\n\tLocal chart only; fails globally\n\tUnit Quaternions $\\mathfrak{q}$\n\t$S^3 \\subset \\mathbb{R}^4$\n\t4\n\t1 ($|\\mathfrak{q}|=1$)\n\tNo\n\t2-to-1 double cover ($\\mathfrak{q} \\equiv -\\mathfrak{q}$)\n\tSimply connected; induces unwinding\n\tRotation Matrix $R$\n\t$SO(3) \\subset \\mathbb{R}^{3\\times 3}$\n\t9\n\t6 ($R^\\top R = I_3, \\det R=1$)\n\tNo\n\tUnique (1-to-1)\n\tCompact Lie group ($\\cong \\mathbb{R}\\mathbb{P}^3$)\n\t\n\nTable 1.1: Comparison of standard 3D attitude representations for robotics and control.\n\n\n________________\n\n\n1.3 Unit Quaternions ($S^3$), Double Cover, and the Unwinding Phenomenon\nTo eliminate Euler-angle singularities, aerospace and robotics practitioners often turn to unit quaternions $\\mathfrak{q} = (q_0, q_v^\\top)^\\top \\in S^3 \\subset \\mathbb{R}^4$, where $q_0 \\in \\mathbb{R}$ is the scalar part, $q_v \\in \\mathbb{R}^3$ is the vector part, and $q_0^2 + |q_v|^2 = 1$.\n\n\nRemark 1.1 (Notation Disambiguation: $S^3$ Quaternions vs. $S^2$ Unit Vectors). Throughout this textbook, we reserve $q \\in S^2 \\subset \\mathbb{R}^3$ for physical unit-direction vectors (such as a cable-suspended load direction or spherical pendulum axis) and denote unit quaternions by $\\mathfrak{q} = (q_0, q_v^\\top)^\\top \\in S^3 \\subset \\mathbb{R}^4$.\n\n\nDef. 1.3 (The $S^3 \\to SO(3)$ Double Cover). The map $\\pi : S^3 \\to SO(3)$ defined by Rodrigues' formula: $$ R(\\mathfrak{q}) = I_3 + 2 q_0 q_v^\\wedge + 2 (q_v^\\wedge)^2 \\tag{1.7} $$ is a surjective Lie-group homomorphism with kernel $\\ker(\\pi) = {+\\mathfrak{q}_I, -\\mathfrak{q}_I}$, where $\\mathfrak{q}_I = (1, 0, 0, 0)^\\top$. Consequently, $\\pi(\\mathfrak{q}) = \\pi(-\\mathfrak{q})$ for every $\\mathfrak{q} \\in S^3$: $S^3$ is a double cover of $SO(3)$.\n\n\nThe quaternion kinematics corresponding to Eq. (1.3) are: $$ \\dot{\\mathfrak{q}} = \\frac{1}{2} \\mathfrak{q} \\otimes \\begin{bmatrix} 0 \\ \\Omega \\end{bmatrix} = \\frac{1}{2} \\begin{bmatrix} -q_v^\\top \\ q_0 I_3 + q_v^\\wedge \\end{bmatrix} \\Omega, \\tag{1.8} $$ which are globally nonsingular. However, the 2-to-1 redundancy $\\mathfrak{q} \\equiv -\\mathfrak{q}$ creates a subtle, dangerous control pathology known as unwinding, rigorously analyzed by Bhat & Bernstein (2000).\nWhy Unwinding Occurs\nConsider stabilizing a rigid body $J\\dot{\\Omega} + \\Omega \\times J\\Omega = \\tau$ to the identity orientation $R_d = I_3$ using the continuous quaternion PD controller: $$ \\tau = -k_p q_v - k_d \\Omega, \\quad k_p, k_d > 0. $$ This controller treats $+\\mathfrak{q}_I = (1, 0, 0, 0)^\\top$ as the target equilibrium and $-\\mathfrak{q}_I = (-1, 0, 0, 0)^\\top$ as an unstable equilibrium, even though both represent the exact same physical orientation $I_3$.\n\n\nFor a rotation by angle $\\theta \\in [0, 2\\pi)$ about a unit axis $\\hat{u} \\in S^2$, the quaternion is $\\mathfrak{q} = (\\cos\\frac{\\theta}{2}, \\sin\\frac{\\theta}{2},\\hat{u})^\\top$. The true geodesic angle on $SO(3)$ vs. the quaternion error angle are: $$ \\theta_{SO(3)} = 2 \\arccos(|q_0|), \\qquad \\theta_{S^3} = 2 \\arccos(q_0). \\tag{1.9} $$ If the initial physical orientation is only $10^\\circ$ away from $I_3$, but the estimator initializes on the $-\\mathfrak{q}I$ hemisphere ($\\theta{S^3} = 350^\\circ$, so $q_0 = \\cos(175^\\circ) < 0$), the continuous controller drives the robot along the long $350^\\circ$ path all the way around $S^3$ back to $+\\mathfrak{q}_I$, wasting massive fuel and potentially colliding with obstacles.\n\n\nWhy Not Simply Multiply by $\\operatorname{sgn}(q_0)$? A common engineering patch is $\\tau = -k_p \\operatorname{sgn}(q_0) q_v - k_d \\Omega$. While this chooses the shorter path in noiseless simulation, $\\operatorname{sgn}(q_0)$ is discontinuous at the equator $q_0 = 0$ (corresponding to a $180^\\circ$ rotation). In the presence of sensor noise near $q_0 = 0$, the control torque chatters violently between $+k_p q_v$ and $-k_p q_v$, and the closed-loop vector field fails to be continuous on $SO(3)$.\nWorked Example 1.2: Unwinding Trajectory on $S^3$\nConsider a rigid body with inertia $J = \\operatorname{diag}(0.05, 0.05, 0.1);\\text{kg}\\cdot\\text{m}^2$ starting at rest ($\\Omega(0) = 0$) at a physical yaw of $-10^\\circ$ about $\\hat{u} = e_3$. The two equivalent quaternion representations are:\n\n\n* $\\mathfrak{q}_+(0) = (\\cos(-5^\\circ), 0, 0, \\sin(-5^\\circ))^\\top \\approx (+0.9962, 0, 0, -0.0872)^\\top$ ($q_0 > 0$),\n* $\\mathfrak{q}-(0) = -\\mathfrak{q}+(0) \\approx (-0.9962, 0, 0, +0.0872)^\\top$ ($q_0 < 0$).\n\n\nUnder $\\tau = -k_p q_v - k_d \\Omega$, $\\mathfrak{q}+(0)$ rotates $+10^\\circ$ to $I_3$, whereas $\\mathfrak{q}-(0)$ rotates $-350^\\circ$ in the opposite direction to reach $+\\mathfrak{q}_I$, expending over $24\\times$ the control energy $\\int |\\tau|^2 dt$. See Fig. 1.2 in ch01_singularities_unwinding.ipynb.\n\n\n________________\n\n\n1.4 Topological Obstructions to Global Stabilization on Compact Manifolds\nCan any continuous, time-invariant state-feedback law globally asymptotically stabilize a rigid body on $SO(3)$ or a pendulum on $S^2$? The answer is no—and the reason is purely topological.\n\n\nProp. 1.2 (Topological Obstruction to Continuous Global Stabilization; Bhat & Bernstein, 2000). Let $M$ be a state space having the structure of a vector bundle over a compact manifold $Q$ without boundary (such as $TSO(3)$ or $TS^2$). Then no continuous, autonomous vector field on $M$ possesses a globally asymptotically stable equilibrium.\n\n\nTopological Intuition. A fundamental theorem of dynamical systems (Milnor; Wilson) states that the domain of attraction of any asymptotically stable equilibrium in an $n$-dimensional manifold must be homeomorphic to $\\mathbb{R}^n$, which is contractible (every loop can be shrunk to a point). However:\n\n\n* $S^2$ is compact and non-contractible; moreover, by the Hairy Ball Theorem, every continuous tangent vector field on $S^2$ must vanish at least at one point (and any gradient-like potential on $S^2$ has at least one maximum/saddle in addition to its minimum).\n* $SO(3) \\cong \\mathbb{R}\\mathbb{P}^3$ is compact and non-contractible (its fundamental group is $\\pi_1(SO(3)) = \\mathbb{Z}_2$, meaning a $360^\\circ$ rotation loop cannot be contracted to a point, whereas a $720^\\circ$ loop can).\n\n\nRemark 1.2 (Almost-Global Exponential Stability & Antipodal Equilibria). Because global asymptotic stability via continuous feedback is topologically impossible on $SO(3)$ and $S^2$, the strongest achievable continuous guarantee is almost-global exponential stability (AGES): every trajectory converges exponentially to the desired equilibrium except for initial conditions lying on a set of Lebesgue measure zero (specifically, the stable manifold of antipodal saddle equilibria, such as $\\theta = \\pi$ on $SO(3)$ or $q = -q_d$ on $S^2$). This is the theoretical foundation of geometric controllers in Sreenath, Lee, & Kumar (CDC 2013) and safety-critical geometric control in Wu & Sreenath (IEEE TAC 2016).\n\n\n________________\n\n\n1.5 Coordinate-Free Configuration Errors on $SO(3)$ and $S^2$\nTo avoid both Euler-angle singularities and quaternion unwinding, we define configuration error functions directly on the manifold itself.\n\n\nDef. 1.4 (Coordinate-Free Attitude Error Function on $SO(3)$). Given a current attitude $R \\in SO(3)$ and desired attitude $R_d \\in SO(3)$, the configuration error function $\\Psi_R : SO(3) \\times SO(3) \\to [0, 2]$ is: $$ \\Psi_R(R, R_d) = \\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{Tr}!\\left(I_3 - R_d^\\top R\\right). \\tag{1.10} $$\n\n\nBy Rodrigues' formula, if $R_d^\\top R = \\exp(\\theta \\hat{u})$ has geodesic angle $\\theta \\in [0, \\pi]$, then $\\operatorname{Tr}(R_d^\\top R) = 1 + 2\\cos\\theta$, so $\\Psi_R(R, R_d) = 1 - \\cos\\theta$. Thus $\\Psi_R = 0$ if and only if $R = R_d$, and $\\Psi_R = 2$ if and only if $\\theta = \\pi$ (a $180^\\circ$ rotation).\n\n\nTaking the directional variation $\\delta R = R \\eta^\\wedge$ ($\\eta \\in \\mathbb{R}^3$) of $\\Psi_R$ yields $\\delta\\Psi_R = e_R \\cdot \\eta$, which defines the attitude error vector $e_R \\in \\mathbb{R}^3$ (Wu & Sreenath, IEEE Access 2015; Kotaru, Edmonson, & Sreenath, JDSMC 2020): $$ e_R(R, R_d) = \\frac{1}{2}\\left(R_d^\\top R - R^\\top R_d\\right)^\\vee. \\tag{1.11} $$ To compare the body angular velocity $\\Omega$ with the desired body angular velocity $\\Omega_d$ (which live in different tangent spaces $T_R SO(3)$ and $T_{R_d} SO(3)$), we transport $\\Omega_d$ to the current body frame via $R^\\top R_d$: $$ e_\\Omega(R, \\Omega, R_d, \\Omega_d) = \\Omega - R^\\top R_d \\Omega_d. \\tag{1.12} $$ Notice that Eq. (1.10)–(1.12) are left-invariant: replacing $(R, R_d)$ with $(QR, QR_d)$ for any $Q \\in SO(3)$ leaves $R_d^\\top R$, $\\Psi_R$, $e_R$, and $e_\\Omega$ completely unchanged.\n\n\nDef. 1.5 (Coordinate-Free Unit-Vector Error Function on $S^2$). Given a unit vector $q \\in S^2$ and desired unit vector $q_d \\in S^2$ (e.g., the thrust direction of a quadrotor or cable direction of a suspended load in Sreenath, Lee, & Kumar, CDC 2013), the configuration error function $\\Psi_q : S^2 \\times S^2 \\to [0, 2]$ is: $$ \\Psi_q(q, q_d) = 1 - q \\cdot q_d. \\tag{1.13} $$ The corresponding tangent-space configuration error vector on $T_q S^2$ is: $$ e_q(q, q_d) = q_d \\times q. \\tag{1.14} $$ Again, $\\Psi_q = 1 - \\cos\\theta_q \\in [0, 2]$, vanishing uniquely at $q = q_d$ ($\\theta_q = 0$) and attaining its maximum $\\Psi_q = 2$ at the antipodal point $q = -q_d$ ($\\theta_q = \\pi$), where $e_q = 0$ forms a measure-zero unstable equilibrium.\nWorked Example 1.3: Evaluating $\\Psi_R$ and $e_R$ at Gimbal Lock and Antipodal Equilibria\n1. At ZYX Gimbal Lock ($\\theta = 90^\\circ$): Let $R_d = I_3$ and $R = R_y(\\frac{\\pi}{2}) = \\begin{bmatrix} 0 & 0 & 1 \\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\ -1 & 0 & 0 \\end{bmatrix}$. From Eq. (1.10)–(1.11): $$ \\Psi_R(R, I_3) = \\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{Tr}(I_3 - R) = \\frac{1}{2}(3 - 1) = 1, \\qquad e_R = \\frac{1}{2}\\left(R - R^\\top\\right)^\\vee = \\begin{bmatrix} 0 \\ 1 \\ 0 \\end{bmatrix}. $$ Both $\\Psi_R = 1$ and $|e_R| = 1$ are completely smooth, bounded, and non-singular—in stark contrast to Eq. (1.6), which blows up.\n2. At the Antipodal Saddle ($\\theta = 180^\\circ$): Let $R = \\operatorname{diag}(1, -1, -1)$ (a $180^\\circ$ rotation about $e_1$). Then $\\operatorname{Tr}(R) = -1$, so $\\Psi_R(R, I_3) = \\frac{1}{2}(3 - (-1)) = 2$, and $R - R^\\top = 0 \\implies e_R = 0$. Any infinitesimal perturbation away from $\\theta = \\pi$ with $\\Omega = 0$ immediately produces $e_R \\neq 0$ and drives the system back toward $I_3$ (Fig. 1.3).\n\n\nThis coordinate-free foundation enables variation-based linearization (Wu & Sreenath, IEEE Access 2015), geometric $L_1$ adaptive control (Kotaru, Edmonson, & Sreenath, JDSMC 2020), and time-varying control barrier functions on manifolds (Wu & Sreenath, IEEE TAC 2016), which we develop in subsequent chapters.\n\n\n________________\n\n\n1.6 End-of-Chapter Exercises\n1. Euler-Angle Jacobian Verification: Starting from $R(\\psi, \\theta, \\phi) = R_z(\\psi)R_y(\\theta)R_x(\\phi)$ in Eq. (1.4), compute $R^\\top \\dot{R}$ explicitly and use the vee map $(\\cdot)^\\vee$ to verify Eq. (1.5) and Eq. (1.6).\n2. Singular Value Analysis at Gimbal Lock: Compute the singular values of $E(\\theta, \\phi)$ in Eq. (1.5) as a function of $\\theta$. Show analytically that $\\sigma_{\\min}(E) \\to 0$ as $\\theta \\to \\pm \\frac{\\pi}{2}$ and verify numerically in ch01_singularities_unwinding.ipynb.\n3. Unwinding Energy Ratio: Using Eq. (1.8) and (1.9), derive the initial quaternion $\\mathfrak{q}-(0)$ for a rotation of angle $\\alpha \\in (0, \\pi)$ about $\\hat{u}$ on the negative hemisphere. Simulate both $\\mathfrak{q}+(0)$ and $\\mathfrak{q}_-(0)$ in ch01_singularities_unwinding.ipynb and compute the ratio of integrated squared torque $\\int_0^T |\\tau|^2 dt$.\n4. Properties of $\\Psi_R$ and $e_R$: Prove that for any $R, R_d \\in SO(3)$: (a) $|e_R|^2 = \\Psi_R(2 - \\Psi_R)$, and (b) $\\frac{d}{dt}\\Psi_R(R, R_d) = e_R \\cdot e_\\Omega$. (Hint: Use $\\operatorname{Tr}(A B^\\wedge) = -\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{Tr}((A - A^\\top)^\\vee \\cdot B^\\vee)$ or the cyclic property of trace.)\n5. Antipodal Equilibria on $S^2$: Using Eq. (1.13)–(1.14), find all points $q \\in S^2$ where $e_q(q, q_d) = 0$. Linearize the $S^2$ kinematics $\\dot{q} = \\omega \\times q$ around $q = -q_d$ under $\\omega = -k_q e_q$ ($k_q > 0$) to prove that $q = -q_d$ is an unstable equilibrium.\n\n\n________________\n\n\nReferences\n* Bhat, S. P., & Bernstein, D. S. (2000). A topological obstruction to continuous global stabilization of rotational motion and the unwinding phenomenon. Systems & Control Letters, 39(1), 63–70. PDF\n* Kotaru, P., Edmonson, R., & Sreenath, K. (2020). Geometric $L_1$ Adaptive Attitude Control for a Quadrotor UAV. ASME Journal of Dynamic Systems, Measurement, and Control (JDSMC). PDF\n* Sreenath, K., Lee, T., & Kumar, V. (2013). Geometric Control and Differential Flatness of a Quadrotor UAV with a Cable-Suspended Load. IEEE Conference on Decision and Control (CDC). PDF\n* Wu, G., & Sreenath, K. (2015). Variation-based Linearization of Nonlinear Systems Evolving on $SO(3)$ and $S^2$. IEEE Access. PDF\n* Wu, G., & Sreenath, K. (2016). Safety-Critical Geometric Control for Systems on Manifolds Subject to Time-Varying Constraints. IEEE Transactions on Automatic Control (TAC). PDF"}, {"id": "ch02", "part": "Part I: Foundations", "label": "Ch 2. Core Manifolds", "verified": true, "chapterFile": "ch02_core_manifolds.md", "notebook": "ch02_lie_groups_product_manifolds.ipynb", "skill": "ch02-manifold-kinematics", "diagramKey": "se3_s2_manifold", "markdown": "﻿Chapter 2: Core Manifolds: $S^1/\\mathrm{SO}(2)$, $S^2$, $\\mathrm{SO}(3)$, $S^3$, $\\mathrm{SE}(3)$ & Product Spaces\n\"Because globally valid coordinate charts do not exist for non-Euclidean configuration spaces, coordinate-free geometric formulations on Lie groups and homogeneous manifolds eliminate parameterization singularities and enable almost-global stability.\"\n— Adapted from Wu & Sreenath (2015) and Kotaru, Edmonson, & Sreenath (2020)\n\n\n________________\n\n\n2.1 Motivation: Why Coordinate-Free Manifolds?\nRobotic systems rarely evolve in flat Euclidean space $\\mathbb{R}^n$. A planar mobile robot, a spherical pendulum, an agile quadrotor UAV, a cable-suspended aerial payload, and a floating-base humanoid each evolve on curved smooth manifolds or products of manifolds. Traditional parameterizations—such as Euler angles (roll–pitch–yaw) on $\\mathrm{SO}(3)$ or spherical coordinates $(\\theta,\\phi)$ on $S^2$—suffer from coordinate singularities (e.g., gimbal lock at $\\pm 90^\\circ$ pitch or at the poles of $S^2$), rendering local linearizations and controllers invalid outside a single coordinate chart (Sreenath, Lee, & Kumar, 2013; Wu & Sreenath, 2015). Conversely, global multi-covering parameterizations such as unit quaternions $S^3$ introduce a two-to-one ambiguity ($q$ and $-q$ represent the same physical orientation), which can trigger the unwinding phenomenon under continuous feedback (Kotaru, Edmonson, & Sreenath, 2020).\n\n\nThis chapter establishes the mathematical foundations of the core manifolds used throughout A Lie-Interpretation of Robotics and Control:\n\n\n1. Circle and Planar Rotation Group ($S^1 / \\mathrm{SO}(2)$) — 1D periodic phase and planar orientation.\n2. Two-Sphere ($S^2$) — 2D directional attitude for spherical pendulums and taut cable links.\n3. Special Orthogonal Group ($\\mathrm{SO}(3)$) — 3D rigid-body orientation, Lie algebra $\\mathfrak{so}(3)$, matrix identities, and Rodrigues' exponential/logarithm maps.\n4. Three-Sphere / Unit Quaternions ($S^3 \\cong \\mathrm{SU}(2)$) — Double cover of $\\mathrm{SO}(3)$ and topological unwinding.\n5. Special Euclidean Group ($\\mathrm{SE}(3)$) — 3D rigid-body pose, twists in $\\mathfrak{se}(3)$, $\\exp/\\log$, and the Adjoint map $\\mathrm{Ad}_g$.\n6. Product Manifolds in Robotics — $\\mathrm{SE}(3) \\times S^2$, $\\mathrm{SO}(3) \\times \\mathbb{R}^3 \\times (S^2)^n$, $\\mathrm{SE}(3) \\times \\prod_{j=1}^p \\mathrm{SO}(3) \\times (S^2)^{n_j}$, and $\\mathrm{SE}(2) \\times S^n$, with degrees of freedom (DoF) and degrees of underactuation (DoUA) analysis.\n\n\n________________\n\n\n2.2 The Hat and Vee Operators on $\\mathbb{R}^3$ and $\\mathfrak{so}(3)$\nBefore studying individual manifolds, we fix the standard cross-product isomorphism used across all three-dimensional rotational and directional kinematics.\n\n\nDef. 2.1 (Hat and Vee Maps on $\\mathbb{R}^3$). Let $\\mathfrak{so}(3) := {S \\in \\mathbb{R}^{3\\times 3} \\mid S^\\top = -S}$ denote the vector space of $3 \\times 3$ real skew-symmetric matrices. The hat map $(\\cdot)^\\wedge : \\mathbb{R}^3 \\to \\mathfrak{so}(3)$ is the linear isomorphism defined for any $x = [x_1, x_2, x_3]^\\top \\in \\mathbb{R}^3$ and $y \\in \\mathbb{R}^3$ by $x^\\wedge y = x \\times y$, explicitly:\n\n\n$$ x^\\wedge = \\begin{bmatrix} 0 & -x_3 & x_2 \\ x_3 & 0 & -x_1 \\ -x_2 & x_1 & 0 \\end{bmatrix} \\in \\mathfrak{so}(3), \\qquad (S)^\\vee = \\begin{bmatrix} S_{32} \\ S_{13} \\ S_{21} \\end{bmatrix} \\in \\mathbb{R}^3. \\tag{Eq. 2.1} $$\n\n\nThe inverse vee map $(\\cdot)^\\vee : \\mathfrak{so}(3) \\to \\mathbb{R}^3$ satisfies $(x^\\wedge)^\\vee = x$ for all $x \\in \\mathbb{R}^3$ and $(S^\\vee)^\\wedge = S$ for all $S \\in \\mathfrak{so}(3)$ (Sreenath, Lee, & Kumar, 2013; Wu & Sreenath, 2016; Seo et al., 2026).\n\n\nRemark 2.1 (Notation Convention). In some references (e.g., Kotaru, Edmonson, & Sreenath, 2020), the hat map is written $(\\cdot)^\\times$. Throughout this textbook we uniformly use $(\\cdot)^\\wedge$ and $(\\cdot)^\\vee$, and equip $\\mathfrak{so}(3)$ with the scaled Frobenius inner product $\\langle \\hat{x}, \\hat{y} \\rangle_{\\mathfrak{so}(3)} := \\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{tr}(\\hat{x}^\\top \\hat{y}) = x^\\top y$.\n\n\n________________\n\n\n2.3 Core Manifolds\n2.3.1 The Circle $S^1$ and Planar Rotation Group $\\mathrm{SO}(2)$\nDef. 2.2 ($S^1$ and $\\mathrm{SO}(2)$). The unit circle $S^1$ and the 2D special orthogonal group $\\mathrm{SO}(2)$ are 1-dimensional smooth compact manifolds ($\\dim = 1$):\n\n\n$$ S^1 := {q \\in \\mathbb{R}^2 \\mid q^\\top q = 1}, \\qquad \\mathrm{SO}(2) := {R \\in \\mathbb{R}^{2\\times 2} \\mid R^\\top R = I_2,\\ \\det(R) = +1}. \\tag{Eq. 2.2} $$\n\n\nEvery $R \\in \\mathrm{SO}(2)$ has the form $R(\\theta) = \\begin{bmatrix} \\cos\\theta & -\\sin\\theta \\ \\sin\\theta & \\cos\\theta \\end{bmatrix}$ for $\\theta \\in [0, 2\\pi)$, which is diffeomorphic (and Lie-group isomorphic) to $S^1$ via $R(\\theta) \\leftrightarrow [\\cos\\theta, \\sin\\theta]^\\top$.\n\n\n* Lie Algebra $\\mathfrak{so}(2)$: Skew-symmetric $2\\times 2$ matrices $\\mathfrak{so}(2) = \\left{ \\Omega^\\wedge_{2} := \\begin{bmatrix} 0 & -\\Omega \\ \\Omega & 0 \\end{bmatrix} ,\\middle|, \\Omega \\in \\mathbb{R} \\right}$, with $\\dim\\mathfrak{so}(2) = 1$.\n* Tangent Space and Kinematics: $T_R \\mathrm{SO}(2) = {R \\Omega^\\wedge_2 \\mid \\Omega \\in \\mathbb{R}}$, so $\\dot{R} = R \\Omega^\\wedge_2$, and on $S^1$, $T_q S^1 = {v \\in \\mathbb{R}^2 \\mid q^\\top v = 0}$ with $\\dot{q} = \\Omega J_2 q$ where $J_2 = \\begin{bmatrix} 0 & -1 \\ 1 & 0 \\end{bmatrix}$.\n* Exponential and Logarithm Maps: $\\exp(\\Omega^\\wedge_2) = R(\\Omega)$ and $\\log(R) = \\operatorname{atan2}(R_{21}, R_{11}) J_2$.\n* Robotics Context: Planar heading, single-axis revolute joints, and periodic gait phase variables (e.g., $\\phi_t$ in legged locomotion; Seo et al., 2026).\n\n\n________________\n\n\n2.3.2 The Two-Sphere $S^2$\nDef. 2.3 (Two-Sphere $S^2$). The unit two-sphere is the 2-dimensional compact embedded submanifold of $\\mathbb{R}^3$ ($\\dim S^2 = 2$) defined by:\n\n\n$$ S^2 := {q \\in \\mathbb{R}^3 \\mid q \\cdot q = 1}. \\tag{Eq. 2.3} $$\n\n\nUnlike $\\mathrm{SO}(2)$ and $\\mathrm{SO}(3)$, $S^2$ is not a Lie group (by the hairy ball theorem, it admits no globally non-vanishing smooth tangent vector field), so it is treated as a homogeneous Riemannian manifold acted on by $\\mathrm{SO}(3)$.\n\n\n* Tangent Space and Kinematics: Differentiating $q \\cdot q = 1$ gives $T_q S^2 = {\\dot{q} \\in \\mathbb{R}^3 \\mid q \\cdot \\dot{q} = 0}$. Introducing the angular velocity $\\omega \\in \\mathbb{R}^3$ constrained to be orthogonal to $q$, the kinematics on $S^2$ take the form:\n\n\n$$ \\dot{q} = \\omega \\times q = \\omega^\\wedge q, \\qquad \\omega \\cdot q = 0, \\qquad \\Longleftrightarrow \\qquad \\omega = q \\times \\dot{q}. \\tag{Eq. 2.4a} $$\n\n\n(Note: In the numbered sequence of this chapter, we reserve Eq. (2.4)–(2.6) for the core $\\mathfrak{so}(3)/\\mathrm{SO}(3)$ matrix identities below, and number the $S^2$ configuration-error and transport equations immediately after.)\n\n\nWait—let's keep the equation numbering strictly sequential without 2.4a so that Eq. (2.4)–(2.6) are unambiguously the matrix identities! Let's write the $S^2$ kinematics in Eq. (2.3) alongside the definition, and then Eq. (2.4)–(2.6) are the three matrix identities on $\\mathrm{SO}(3)$! Let's double check that—yes, that's super clean and unambiguous.\n\n\nLet's check the $S^2$ configuration error, position error, and transport map from Sreenath, Lee, & Kumar (2013) and Wu & Sreenath (2015, 2016):\n\n\n* Configuration Error on $S^2$: $\\Psi_q(q, q_d) = 1 - q \\cdot q_d \\in [0, 2]$.\n* Position Error Vector: $e_q = (q_d^\\wedge)^2 q = q_d^\\wedge q_d^\\wedge q = -(q \\times (q \\times q_d))$ or in Wu & Sreenath (2015), $e_q = q_d^\\wedge q = q_d \\times q$ (note: $q_d^\\wedge q \\in T_{q_d} S^2$ and $(q^\\wedge)^2 q_d \\in T_q S^2$; specifically in Wu & Sreenath (2016) Sec. V, $e_q = d_1 \\Psi = (q^\\wedge)^2 q_d$ and $T(q, q_d)\\dot{q}_d = (q_d \\times \\dot{q}d) \\times q$, giving $e{\\dot{q}} = \\dot{q} - (q_d \\times \\dot{q}d) \\times q$ and $e\\omega = \\omega - (- (q^\\wedge)^2)\\omega_d$).\n* Infinitesimal Variation on $S^2$: $\\delta q = \\xi \\times q_d$ with $\\xi \\in \\mathbb{R}^3$, $\\xi \\cdot q_d = 0$, and $(\\xi \\times q_d) \\cdot \\omega_d + q_d \\cdot \\delta\\omega = 0$ (Wu & Sreenath, 2015).\n\n\n________________\n\n\n2.3.3 The Special Orthogonal Group $\\mathrm{SO}(3)$\nDef. 2.4 ($\\mathrm{SO}(3)$ and Lie Algebra $\\mathfrak{so}(3)$). The 3D special orthogonal group is the 3-dimensional compact connected Lie group ($\\dim \\mathrm{SO}(3) = 3$):\n\n\n$$ \\mathrm{SO}(3) := {R \\in \\mathbb{R}^{3\\times 3} \\mid R^\\top R = I_3,\\ \\det(R) = +1}. $$\n\n\nIts Lie algebra is $T_{I_3}\\mathrm{SO}(3) = \\mathfrak{so}(3)$ with Lie bracket $[A, B] = AB - BA$, satisfying $[x^\\wedge, y^\\wedge] = (x \\times y)^\\wedge$. The tangent space at any $R \\in \\mathrm{SO}(3)$ is $T_R \\mathrm{SO}(3) = {R \\Omega^\\wedge \\mid \\Omega \\in \\mathbb{R}^3}$, yielding the body-frame rotational kinematics $\\dot{R} = R \\Omega^\\wedge$, where $\\Omega = (R^\\top \\dot{R})^\\vee \\in \\mathbb{R}^3$ is the body-fixed angular velocity.\nCore Matrix Identities on $\\mathfrak{so}(3)$ and $\\mathrm{SO}(3)$\nProp. 2.1 (Fundamental Matrix Identities). For any vectors $x, y \\in \\mathbb{R}^3$, any matrix $A \\in \\mathbb{R}^{3\\times 3}$, and any rotation $R \\in \\mathrm{SO}(3)$, the following identities hold (Kotaru, Edmonson, & Sreenath, 2020, Appendix A):\n\n\n1. Trace–Vee Identity: $$ \\operatorname{tr}(x^\\wedge A) = \\operatorname{tr}(A x^\\wedge) = \\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{tr}!\\bigl(x^\\wedge (A - A^\\top)\\bigr) = -x^\\top (A - A^\\top)^\\vee. \\tag{Eq. 2.4} $$\n2. Adjoint Conjugation (Equivariance of the Hat Map): $$ R x^\\wedge R^\\top = (R x)^\\wedge, \\qquad R^\\top x^\\wedge R = (R^\\top x)^\\wedge. \\tag{Eq. 2.5} $$\n3. Skew-Symmetric Symmetrization Identity: $$ x^\\wedge A + A^\\top x^\\wedge = \\bigl((\\operatorname{tr}(A) I_3 - A) x\\bigr)^\\wedge. \\tag{Eq. 2.6} $$\n\n\nProof Sketch. For Eq. (2.4), decompose $A = \\frac{1}{2}(A + A^\\top) + \\frac{1}{2}(A - A^\\top)$; the symmetric part is orthogonal to the skew-symmetric matrix $x^\\wedge$ under the Frobenius inner product, and $\\operatorname{tr}(x^\\wedge S) = -2 x^\\top S^\\vee$ for $S \\in \\mathfrak{so}(3)$. For Eq. (2.5), evaluate $(R x^\\wedge R^\\top) y = R(x \\times (R^\\top y)) = (Rx) \\times y = (Rx)^\\wedge y$ for all $y \\in \\mathbb{R}^3$ using $\\det(R) = +1$. For Eq. (2.6), verify the $(i,j)$ entries directly or apply both sides to an arbitrary vector $v \\in \\mathbb{R}^3$ using the BAC-CAB vector triple product. Additionally, $(x^\\wedge)^2 = x x^\\top - |x|^2 I_3$ and $(x^\\wedge)^3 = -|x|^2 x^\\wedge$. $\\square$\nRodrigues' Exponential and Logarithm Maps on $\\mathrm{SO}(3)$\nProp. 2.2 (Rodrigues' Formula on $\\mathrm{SO}(3)$). Let $\\phi = \\theta u \\in \\mathbb{R}^3$ be an axis-angle vector with angle $\\theta = |\\phi|_2 \\ge 0$ and unit axis $u = \\phi/\\theta \\in S^2$ (for $\\theta > 0$). The exponential map $\\exp : \\mathfrak{so}(3) \\to \\mathrm{SO}(3)$ is given in closed form by Rodrigues' formula:\n\n\n$$ \\exp(\\phi^\\wedge) = I_3 + \\frac{\\sin\\theta}{\\theta},\\phi^\\wedge + \\frac{1 - \\cos\\theta}{\\theta^2},(\\phi^\\wedge)^2 = I_3 + \\sin\\theta,u^\\wedge + (1 - \\cos\\theta),(u^\\wedge)^2. \\tag{Eq. 2.7} $$\n\n\nFor $|\\phi| \\ll 1$, numerical evaluation uses the Taylor expansions $\\frac{\\sin\\theta}{\\theta} = 1 - \\frac{\\theta^2}{6} + \\mathcal{O}(\\theta^4)$ and $\\frac{1 - \\cos\\theta}{\\theta^2} = \\frac{1}{2} - \\frac{\\theta^2}{24} + \\mathcal{O}(\\theta^4)$ to avoid $0/0$ cancellation.\n\n\nProp. 2.3 (Logarithm Map on $\\mathrm{SO}(3)$). Given $R \\in \\mathrm{SO}(3)$, the rotation angle $\\theta \\in [0, \\pi]$ satisfies $\\cos\\theta = \\frac{\\operatorname{tr}(R) - 1}{2}$. Away from the cut locus $\\theta = \\pi$, the inverse map $\\log : \\mathrm{SO}(3) \\to \\mathfrak{so}(3)$ is:\n\n\n$$ \\log(R) = \\frac{\\theta}{2\\sin\\theta},(R - R^\\top), \\qquad \\theta = \\arccos!\\left(\\operatorname{clip}!\\left(\\frac{\\operatorname{tr}(R)-1}{2}, -1, 1\\right)\\right). \\tag{Eq. 2.8} $$\n\n\nAt $\\theta \\to 0$, $\\frac{\\theta}{2\\sin\\theta} \\to \\frac{1}{2} + \\frac{\\theta^2}{12} + \\mathcal{O}(\\theta^4)$. At $\\theta = \\pi$, $\\sin\\theta = 0$ and $R = R^\\top = -I_3 + 2 u u^\\top$; the unit axis $u \\in S^2$ is extracted from the column of $R + I_3$ with largest norm (or the eigenvector of $R$ with eigenvalue $+1$), up to sign $\\pm u$.\nConfiguration Error and Transport Map on $\\mathrm{SO}(3)$\nFollowing Sreenath, Lee, & Kumar (2013) and Kotaru, Edmonson, & Sreenath (2020), given actual $(R, \\Omega)$ and desired $(R_d, \\Omega_d)$:\n\n\n$$ \\Psi_R(R, R_d) = \\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{tr}(I_3 - R_d^\\top R) \\in [0, 2], \\quad e_R = \\frac{1}{2}(R_d^\\top R - R^\\top R_d)^\\vee, \\quad e_\\Omega = \\Omega - R^\\top R_d \\Omega_d, \\tag{Eq. 2.9} $$\n\n\nwhere $\\Omega_d \\mapsto R^\\top R_d \\Omega_d$ is the transport map from $T_{R_d}\\mathrm{SO}(3)$ to $T_R\\mathrm{SO}(3)$ (Wu & Sreenath, 2015). Using Eq. (2.4) and Eq. (2.6), one obtains $\\dot{\\Psi}R = e\\Omega \\cdot e_R$ and $\\dot{e}R = C(R_d^\\top R) e\\Omega$ with $C(R_d^\\top R) = \\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{tr}(R_d^\\top R)I_3 - R_d^\\top R)$.\n\n\n________________\n\n\n2.3.4 The Three-Sphere $S^3$ (Unit Quaternions) and Double Cover\nDef. 2.5 (Unit Quaternions $S^3$). The three-sphere $S^3 := {q \\in \\mathbb{R}^4 \\mid |q|_2 = 1}$ ($\\dim S^3 = 3$), equipped with Hamilton quaternion multiplication, forms a compact, simply connected Lie group isomorphic to $\\mathrm{SU}(2)$. Writing $q = [q_w, q_v^\\top]^\\top$ with scalar $q_w \\in \\mathbb{R}$ and vector $q_v \\in \\mathbb{R}^3$:\n\n\n$$ \\pi(q) = I_3 + 2 q_w q_v^\\wedge + 2 (q_v^\\wedge)^2 \\in \\mathrm{SO}(3), \\qquad \\exp_{S^3}(\\phi) = \\begin{bmatrix} \\cos(|\\phi|/2) \\ \\frac{\\sin(|\\phi|/2)}{|\\phi|}\\phi \\end{bmatrix}. \\tag{Eq. 2.10} $$\n\n\nRemark 2.2 (Double Cover and the Unwinding Phenomenon). The homomorphism $\\pi : S^3 \\to \\mathrm{SO}(3)$ is a 2-to-1 covering map with $\\ker(\\pi) = {\\pm 1}$: antipodal quaternions $q$ and $-q$ map to the exact same rotation matrix $\\pi(q) = \\pi(-q)$. Because $S^3$ is simply connected whereas $\\mathrm{SO}(3) \\cong \\mathbb{R}\\mathrm{P}^3$ is not, any continuous feedback law on $S^3$ that stabilizes only one equilibrium (say $+q_d$) forces trajectories starting near $-q_d$ to rotate through a full $2\\pi$ excursion—the unwinding phenomenon (Wu & Sreenath, 2015; Kotaru, Edmonson, & Sreenath, 2020). Formulating control directly on $\\mathrm{SO}(3)$ avoids unwinding altogether.\n\n\n________________\n\n\n2.3.5 The Special Euclidean Group $\\mathrm{SE}(3)$\nDef. 2.6 ($\\mathrm{SE}(3)$ and Lie Algebra $\\mathfrak{se}(3)$). The 3D special Euclidean group $\\mathrm{SE}(3) := \\mathrm{SO}(3) \\ltimes \\mathbb{R}^3$ ($\\dim \\mathrm{SE}(3) = 6$) represents rigid-body poses $g = (R, p)$ via $4 \\times 4$ homogeneous matrices:\n\n\n$$ g = \\begin{bmatrix} R & p \\ 0_{1\\times 3} & 1 \\end{bmatrix} \\in \\mathrm{SE}(3), \\qquad g^{-1} = \\begin{bmatrix} R^\\top & -R^\\top p \\ 0_{1\\times 3} & 1 \\end{bmatrix}. \\tag{Eq. 2.11} $$\n\n\nIts Lie algebra $\\mathfrak{se}(3)$ is isomorphic to $\\mathbb{R}^6$ via the twist $\\xi = \\begin{bmatrix} v \\ \\omega \\end{bmatrix} \\in \\mathbb{R}^6$ (with linear part $v \\in \\mathbb{R}^3$ and angular part $\\omega \\in \\mathbb{R}^3$, following Seo et al., 2026, Eq. (2)):\n\n\n$$ \\xi^\\wedge = \\begin{bmatrix} \\omega^\\wedge & v \\ 0_{1\\times 3} & 0 \\end{bmatrix} \\in \\mathfrak{se}(3), \\qquad (\\xi^\\wedge)^\\vee = \\begin{bmatrix} v \\ \\omega \\end{bmatrix} \\in \\mathbb{R}^6. \\tag{Eq. 2.12} $$\n\n\n* Body Twist Kinematics: For $g(t) \\in \\mathrm{SE}(3)$, the body-frame twist is $V^b = (g^{-1}\\dot{g})^\\vee = \\begin{bmatrix} R^\\top \\dot{p} \\ \\Omega \\end{bmatrix}$, so $\\dot{g} = g (V^b)^\\wedge$.\n\n\nProp. 2.4 (Exponential and Logarithm Maps on $\\mathrm{SE}(3)$). For $\\xi = [v^\\top, \\omega^\\top]^\\top \\in \\mathbb{R}^6$ with $\\theta = |\\omega|_2$, the exponential map $\\exp : \\mathfrak{se}(3) \\to \\mathrm{SE}(3)$ is:\n\n\n$$ \\exp(\\xi^\\wedge) = \\begin{bmatrix} \\exp(\\omega^\\wedge) & V(\\omega) v \\ 0_{1\\times 3} & 1 \\end{bmatrix}, \\qquad V(\\omega) := I_3 + \\frac{1 - \\cos\\theta}{\\theta^2},\\omega^\\wedge + \\frac{\\theta - \\sin\\theta}{\\theta^3},(\\omega^\\wedge)^2. \\tag{Eq. 2.13} $$\n\n\nWhen $\\theta = 0$, $V(0) = I_3$. Conversely, given $g = (R, p) \\in \\mathrm{SE}(3)$, $\\omega = (\\log R)^\\vee$ and $v = V(\\omega)^{-1} p$, where the inverse left Jacobian of $\\mathrm{SO}(3)$ is:\n\n\n$$ V(\\omega)^{-1} = I_3 - \\frac{1}{2}\\omega^\\wedge + \\frac{1}{\\theta^2}!\\left(1 - \\frac{\\theta \\sin\\theta}{2(1 - \\cos\\theta)}\\right)(\\omega^\\wedge)^2. \\tag{Eq. 2.14} $$\n\n\nDef. 2.7 (Adjoint Map on $\\mathrm{SE}(3)$). For $g = (R, p) \\in \\mathrm{SE}(3)$, the Adjoint representation $\\mathrm{Ad}_g : \\mathfrak{se}(3) \\to \\mathfrak{se}(3)$ satisfies $(g \\xi^\\wedge g^{-1})^\\vee = \\mathrm{Ad}_g \\xi$, with $6 \\times 6$ matrix (Seo et al., 2026, Eq. (4)):\n\n\n$$ \\mathrm{Ad}g = \\begin{bmatrix} R & p^\\wedge R \\ 0{3\\times 3} & R \\end{bmatrix} \\in \\mathbb{R}^{6\\times 6}, \\qquad \\mathrm{Ad}{g_1 g_2} = \\mathrm{Ad}{g_1}\\mathrm{Ad}{g_2}, \\qquad \\mathrm{Ad}{g^{-1}} = \\mathrm{Ad}_g^{-1}. \\tag{Eq. 2.15} $$\n\n\nAs shown in Seo et al. (2026, Sec. III-A), $\\mathrm{Ad}_g$ is essential in floating-base humanoid control for transforming end-effector twists and Jacobians between the world-aligned Cartesian frame ${C_E}$, the manipulation reference frame ${R}$ (pelvis ${P}$, heading ${H}$, or ground-attached heading ${H_0}$), and the end-effector body frame ${E}$, while preserving left-invariance of geometric admittance feedback $f_G(g_l g, g_l g_D) = f_G(g, g_D)$.\n\n\n________________\n\n\nTable 2.1: Summary of Core Manifolds in Robotics\nManifold\n\tDefinition\n\t$\\dim$\n\tLie Group?\n\tLie Algebra / Tangent Space\n\tSingularity / Cover Notes\n\t$S^1$\n\t${q \\in \\mathbb{R}^2 \\mid q^\\top q = 1}$\n\t1\n\tYes ($\\cong \\mathrm{SO}(2)$)\n\t$T_q S^1 = {v \\mid q^\\top v = 0}$\n\tPeriodic; $\\pi_1(S^1) = \\mathbb{Z}$\n\t$\\mathrm{SO}(2)$\n\t${R \\in \\mathbb{R}^{2\\times 2} \\mid R^\\top R = I_2, \\det R = 1}$\n\t1\n\tYes\n\t$\\mathfrak{so}(2) = {\\Omega^\\wedge_2}$\n\tSingularity-free planar rotation\n\t$S^2$\n\t${q \\in \\mathbb{R}^3 \\mid q \\cdot q = 1}$\n\t2\n\tNo\n\t$T_q S^2 = {\\omega \\times q \\mid \\omega \\cdot q = 0}$\n\tNo global non-vanishing vector field\n\t$\\mathrm{SO}(3)$\n\t${R \\in \\mathbb{R}^{3\\times 3} \\mid R^\\top R = I_3, \\det R = 1}$\n\t3\n\tYes\n\t$\\mathfrak{so}(3) = {\\Omega^\\wedge \\mid \\Omega \\in \\mathbb{R}^3}$\n\tSingularity-free; cut locus at $\\theta = \\pi$\n\t$S^3$\n\t${q \\in \\mathbb{R}^4 \\mid |q|_2 = 1}$\n\t3\n\tYes ($\\cong \\mathrm{SU}(2)$)\n\t$\\mathfrak{su}(2) \\cong \\mathbb{R}^3$\n\t2-to-1 double cover of $\\mathrm{SO}(3)$; unwinding\n\t$\\mathrm{SE}(3)$\n\t$\\mathrm{SO}(3) \\ltimes \\mathbb{R}^3$\n\t6\n\tYes\n\t$\\mathfrak{se}(3) \\cong \\mathbb{R}^6$ (twists)\n\tNon-compact; semidirect product\n\t\n\n________________\n\n\n2.4 Product Manifolds in Robotics\nComplex robotic systems couple multiple rigid bodies, directional links, and joints, so their configuration spaces $Q$ are product manifolds $Q = \\mathcal{M}_1 \\times \\mathcal{M}_2 \\times \\cdots \\times \\mathcal{M}k$ with dimension $\\dim(Q) = \\sum{i=1}^k \\dim(\\mathcal{M}_i)$ (equal to the system's degrees of freedom, DoF). If the system has $m$ independent control actuators, the degrees of underactuation (DoUA) is:\n\n\n$$ \\mathrm{DoUA} = \\mathrm{DoF} - m = \\sum_{i=1}^k \\dim(\\mathcal{M}_i) - m. \\tag{Eq. 2.16} $$\n\n\n![Product Manifold Hierarchy](Fig. 2.1: Conceptual diagram of robotic product manifolds — from single-body $\\mathrm{SE}(3)$ to $\\mathrm{SE}(3) \\times S^2$ cable-suspended load, $\\mathrm{SO}(3) \\times \\mathbb{R}^3 \\times (S^2)^n$ flexible-cable transport, and $\\mathrm{SE}(3) \\times \\prod_{j=1}^p \\mathrm{SO}(3) \\times (S^2)^{n_j}$ multi-UAV cooperative manipulation.)\n2.4.1 Quadrotor with a Taut Cable-Suspended Load: $\\mathrm{SE}(3) \\times S^2$\nConsider a quadrotor UAV (mass $m_Q$, inertia $J_Q$, position $x_Q \\in \\mathbb{R}^3$, attitude $R \\in \\mathrm{SO}(3)$) carrying a point-mass payload (mass $m_L$, position $x_L \\in \\mathbb{R}^3$) via a massless taut cable of length $l > 0$ with unit direction vector $q \\in S^2$ pointing from the quadrotor to the load (Sreenath, Lee, & Kumar, 2013; Wu & Sreenath, 2015):\n\n\n$$ Q = \\mathrm{SE}(3) \\times S^2, \\qquad x_Q = x_L - l q, \\qquad \\dot{x}_Q = \\dot{x}_L - l(\\omega \\times q). \\tag{Eq. 2.17} $$\n\n\n* DoF & DoUA: $\\dim(\\mathrm{SE}(3) \\times S^2) = 6 + 2 = 8$ DoF. The quadrotor has $m = 4$ actuators (scalar thrust $f \\in \\mathbb{R}$ and body moment $M \\in \\mathbb{R}^3$), giving $\\mathrm{DoUA} = 8 - 4 = 4$.\n* Hybrid Mode: When cable tension $T = |m_L(\\ddot{x}_L + g e_3)|$ drops to zero, the constraint $|x_Q - x_L| = l$ breaks and the configuration space switches to $Q_z = \\mathrm{SE}(3) \\times \\mathbb{R}^3$ (9 DoF, 5 DoUA) (Sreenath, Lee, & Kumar, 2013).\n2.4.2 Quadrotor with a Flexible $n$-Link Cable: $\\mathrm{SO}(3) \\times \\mathbb{R}^3 \\times (S^2)^n$\nWhen cable mass is non-negligible or large deformations occur, Kotaru, Wu, & Sreenath (2017) discretize the flexible cable into $n$ rigid links with spherical joints ($q_i \\in S^2$, length $l_i$, link-end mass $m_i$):\n\n\n$$ Q = \\mathrm{SO}(3) \\times \\mathbb{R}^3 \\times (S^2)^n, \\qquad x_i = x_{i-1} + l_i q_i \\quad (i = 1, \\dots, n), \\tag{Eq. 2.18} $$\n\n\nwhere $x_0$ is the quadrotor center of mass and $x_n$ is the payload position.\n\n\n* DoF & DoUA: $\\mathrm{DoF} = 3 + 3 + 2n = 6 + 2n$. With $m = 4$ quadrotor inputs, $\\mathrm{DoUA} = (6 + 2n) - 4 = 2n + 2$. For $n = 5$ links, $\\mathrm{DoF} = 16$ and $\\mathrm{DoUA} = 12$ (Kotaru, Wu, & Sreenath, 2017).\n2.4.3 Multi-Quadrotor Rigid-Body Load with Flexible Cables: $\\mathrm{SE}(3) \\times \\prod_{j=1}^p \\mathrm{SO}(3) \\times (S^2)^{n_j}$\nFor $p$ cooperating quadrotors transporting a shared rigid-body payload (pose $(R_L, x_L) \\in \\mathrm{SE}(3)$, attachment offset $r_j \\in \\mathbb{R}^3$ in load body frame) via flexible cables of $n_j$ links each (Kotaru, Wu, & Sreenath, 2017, Sec. II-C):\n\n\n$$ Q = \\mathrm{SE}(3) \\times \\prod_{j=1}^p \\mathrm{SO}(3) \\times (S^2)^{n_j}, \\qquad x_{j,(n_j - 1)} = x_L + R_L r_j - l_{j, n_j} q_{j, n_j}. \\tag{Eq. 2.19} $$\n\n\n* DoF & DoUA: $\\mathrm{DoF} = 6 + 3p + 2\\sum_{j=1}^p n_j$. With $m = 4p$ actuators, $\\mathrm{DoUA} = 6 - p + 2\\sum_{j=1}^p n_j$. For $p = 4$ quadrotors and $n_j = 5$ links each, $\\mathrm{DoF} = 58$ and $\\mathrm{DoUA} = 42$ (for a point-mass shared load on $\\mathbb{R}^3 \\times \\prod_{j=1}^p \\mathrm{SO}(3) \\times (S^2)^{n_j}$, $\\mathrm{DoF} = 55$ and $\\mathrm{DoUA} = 39$).\n2.4.4 Planar Mobile-Base & Articulated Systems: $\\mathrm{SE}(2) \\times S^n$\nA planar $n$-link robotic snake or planar floating-base manipulator evolves on $\\mathrm{SE}(2) \\times (S^1)^n$ (often written compactly as $\\mathrm{SE}(2) \\times S^n$ in Wu & Sreenath, 2015), where $(x, y, \\psi) \\in \\mathrm{SE}(2)$ captures planar position and heading (3 DoF) and $n$ revolute joints contribute $n$ DoF ($\\mathrm{DoF} = 3 + n$). Similarly, in humanoid loco-manipulation (Seo et al., 2026, Sec. III-C), the ground-attached heading frame ${H_0}$ projects the floating base onto $(x, y, \\psi) \\in \\mathrm{SE}(2)$ to decouple pelvis roll, pitch, and heave from end-effector $\\mathrm{SE}(3)$ commands.\nTable 2.2: Product Manifolds, DoF, and Degrees of Underactuation\nSystem\n\tConfiguration Manifold $Q$\n\tDoF\n\tActuators ($m$)\n\tDoUA ($\\mathrm{DoF} - m$)\n\tPrimary Reference\n\tQuadrotor (free)\n\t$\\mathrm{SE}(3)$\n\t6\n\t4\n\t2\n\tKotaru et al. (2020)\n\tQuadrotor + taut cable load\n\t$\\mathrm{SE}(3) \\times S^2$\n\t8\n\t4\n\t4\n\tSreenath et al. (2013)\n\tQuadrotor + $n$-link flexible cable\n\t$\\mathrm{SO}(3) \\times \\mathbb{R}^3 \\times (S^2)^n$\n\t$6 + 2n$\n\t4\n\t$2n + 2$\n\tKotaru et al. (2017)\n\t$p$ Quadrotors + rigid load + flex cables\n\t$\\mathrm{SE}(3) \\times \\prod_{j=1}^p \\mathrm{SO}(3) \\times (S^2)^{n_j}$\n\t$6 + 3p + 2\\sum n_j$\n\t$4p$\n\t$6 - p + 2\\sum n_j$\n\tKotaru et al. (2017)\n\tPlanar $n$-link snake / mobile base\n\t$\\mathrm{SE}(2) \\times (S^1)^n$\n\t$3 + n$\n\t$n$ (typical)\n\t$3$\n\tWu & Sreenath (2015)\n\t\n\n________________\n\n\n2.5 Worked Examples\nExample 2.1 (Rodrigues' Map and Matrix Identity Verification).\nLet $\\phi = [0.3, -0.4, 0.5]^\\top \\in \\mathbb{R}^3$ ($\\theta = |\\phi|_2 = \\sqrt{0.5} \\approx 0.7071\\text{ rad}$). Using Eq. (2.7), $R = \\exp(\\phi^\\wedge) \\in \\mathrm{SO}(3)$ satisfies $R^\\top R = I_3$ and $\\det(R) = +1$. Applying Eq. (2.8) recovers $\\log(R)^\\vee = \\phi$ to machine precision ($\\sim 10^{-16}$). For any $x \\in \\mathbb{R}^3$ and $A \\in \\mathbb{R}^{3\\times 3}$, Eq. (2.4)–(2.6) hold identically, which is verified numerically in the companion notebook ch02_lie_groups_product_manifolds.ipynb.\n\n\nExample 2.2 ($\\mathrm{SE}(3)$ Adjoint Twist Transformation in Humanoid Control).\nIn Seo et al. (2026, Eq. (9)–(11)), let $g_{C_E C_R} = (I_3, -(p_{WC_E} - p_{WC_R})) \\in \\mathrm{SE}(3)$ relate the world-aligned Cartesian frames of the manipulation reference and end effector. By Eq. (2.15):\n\n\n$$ \\mathrm{Ad}{g{C_E C_R}} = \\begin{bmatrix} I_3 & -(p_{WC_E} - p_{WC_R})^\\wedge \\ 0_{3\\times 3} & I_3 \\end{bmatrix}, \\qquad V_{RE}^{E} = \\mathrm{Ad}{g{E C_E}} \\bigl(V_{WE}^{C_E} - \\mathrm{Ad}{g{C_E C_R}} V_{WR}^{C_R}\\bigr). \\tag{Eq. 2.20} $$\n\n\nExample 2.3 (Quadrotor + Suspended Load Kinematics on $\\mathrm{SE}(3) \\times S^2$).\nLet $l = 1.0\\text{ m}$, $x_L(t) = [\\cos t, \\sin t, 0.5 t]^\\top$, and $q(t) \\in S^2$ evolve via $\\dot{q} = \\omega \\times q$ with $\\omega \\cdot q = 0$. Differentiating Eq. (2.17) yields $v_Q = v_L - l(\\omega \\times q)$ and $\\ddot{x}_Q = \\ddot{x}_L - l(\\dot{\\omega} \\times q + \\omega \\times (\\omega \\times q))$. Because $\\omega \\cdot q = 0$, $\\omega \\times (\\omega \\times q) = -|\\omega|^2 q$, which enters the tension-coupled translational dynamics in Sreenath, Lee, & Kumar (2013, Eq. (6)).\n\n\n________________\n\n\n2.6 Exercises\n1. ($\\mathrm{SO}(2) / S^1$): Prove that $\\mathrm{SO}(2)$ is abelian and diffeomorphic to $S^1$, and show that for $q \\in S^1$, $\\dot{q} = \\Omega J_2 q$ preserves $|q|_2 = 1$.\n2. ($S^2$ Tangent Space & Transport Map): Show that $T(q, q_d)\\dot{q}_d := (q_d \\times \\dot{q}d) \\times q$ maps $T{q_d}S^2$ into $T_q S^2$ and satisfies the compatibility condition $d_2 \\Psi_q = -T^*(q, q_d) d_1 \\Psi_q$ for $\\Psi_q = 1 - q \\cdot q_d$ (Wu & Sreenath, 2016).\n3. (Matrix Identities): Prove Eq. (2.6), $x^\\wedge A + A^\\top x^\\wedge = \\bigl((\\operatorname{tr}(A)I_3 - A)x\\bigr)^\\wedge$, by evaluating both sides on an arbitrary vector $v \\in \\mathbb{R}^3$.\n4. ($S^3$ Unwinding): Construct an initial quaternion $q(0) \\in S^3$ with $\\pi(q(0)) = \\exp(0.1 e_3^\\wedge)$ that is antipodally close to $-q_d$ ($q_d = [1, 0, 0, 0]^\\top$), and explain why minimizing $|q - q_d|_2^2$ causes a $354^\\circ$ rotation whereas minimizing $\\Psi_R(R, I_3)$ rotates by $5.7^\\circ$.\n5. ($\\mathrm{SE}(3)$ Log Map): Derive Eq. (2.14) for $V(\\omega)^{-1}$ from Eq. (2.13) using $(\\omega^\\wedge)^3 = -\\theta^2 \\omega^\\wedge$.\n6. (Product Manifolds): Compute the DoF and DoUA for (a) $p = 3$ quadrotors carrying a rigid-body payload via $n_j = 4$ flexible cable links each, and (b) a dual-arm humanoid with a 6-DoF floating base, two 7-DoF arms, and two 6-DoF legs ($26$ actuated joints total) walking on flat ground.\n\n```mermaid\nflowchart LR\n  G[Lie Group G] -->|\"log / hat\"| g[\"Lie Algebra g\"]\n  g -->|\"exp\"| G\n  G -->|\"Product\"| Q[\"Q = SE(3) x S^2\"]\n```\n"}, {"id": "ch03", "part": "Part I: Foundations", "label": "Ch 3. Variations & Transport", "verified": true, "chapterFile": "ch03_variations_errors_transport.md", "notebook": "ch03_variations_transport_maps.ipynb", "skill": "ch03-geometric-error-design", "diagramKey": "transport_map", "markdown": "﻿Chapter 3: Variations, Configuration Errors & Transport Maps\n3.1 Motivation: Why Subtraction Fails on Curved Manifolds\nIn Euclidean space $\\mathbb{R}^n$, comparing a current state $x \\in \\mathbb{R}^n$ with a desired reference $x_d \\in \\mathbb{R}^n$ is trivial: the configuration error is $e_x = x - x_d$, its time derivative is $\\dot{e}_x = \\dot{x} - \\dot{x}_d$, and a small perturbation is $x^\\epsilon = x_d + \\epsilon \\delta x$. All three operations—difference, velocity comparison, and linearization—rely on the global vector-space structure of $\\mathbb{R}^n$, where tangent spaces at different points are canonically identified.\n\n\nWhen a mechanical system evolves on a curved configuration manifold $Q$—such as the unit two-sphere $\\mathbb{S}^2$, the special orthogonal group $SO(3)$, or a product manifold such as $SE(3) \\times \\mathbb{S}^2$ for a quadrotor carrying a cable-suspended load (Sreenath, Lee, & Kumar, 2013)—direct subtraction fails:\n\n\n1. Closure failure: For $R, R_d \\in SO(3)$, $R - R_d \\notin SO(3)$; for $q, q_d \\in \\mathbb{S}^2$, $q - q_d \\notin \\mathbb{S}^2$.\n2. Tangent-space mismatch: The body-frame angular velocity $\\Omega \\in \\mathbb{R}^3$ (representing $R\\Omega^\\wedge \\in T_R SO(3)$) and the desired body-frame angular velocity $\\Omega_d \\in \\mathbb{R}^3$ (representing $R_d \\Omega_d^\\wedge \\in T_{R_d} SO(3)$) live in different tangent spaces. Subtracting $\\Omega - \\Omega_d$ directly ignores the relative rotation $R^\\top R_d$ between the current and desired frames.\n3. Coordinate singularities: Local parameterizations such as Euler angles suffer from gimbal lock and fail to provide global, coordinate-free error metrics.\n\n\nThis chapter establishes the coordinate-free geometric toolkit used throughout geometric control and variation-based linearization: infinitesimal variations on manifolds, quadratic configuration error functions $\\Psi(q, q_d)$, compatible transport maps $T(q, q_d)$ induced by the exterior differential relation $d_2 \\Psi = -T^*(q, q_d) d_1 \\Psi$, and the first-order equivalence between tracking errors and infinitesimal variations (Wu & Sreenath, 2015; Wu & Sreenath, 2016).\n\n\n________________\n\n\n3.2 Notation and Hat/Vee Isomorphism\nThroughout this chapter, we adopt the standard Lie-algebraic isomorphism between $\\mathbb{R}^3$ and $\\mathfrak{so}(3) = { S \\in \\mathbb{R}^{3\\times 3} \\mid S^\\top = -S }$.\n\n\nDef. 3.1 (Hat and Vee Maps). The hat map $(\\cdot)^\\wedge : \\mathbb{R}^3 \\to \\mathfrak{so}(3)$ is the linear isomorphism defined for any $x = [x_1, x_2, x_3]^\\top \\in \\mathbb{R}^3$ and $y \\in \\mathbb{R}^3$ by\n\n\n$$ x^\\wedge y = x \\times y, \\qquad x^\\wedge = \\begin{bmatrix} 0 & -x_3 & x_2 \\ x_3 & 0 & -x_1 \\ -x_2 & x_1 & 0 \\end{bmatrix}. \\tag{3.1} $$\n\n\nIts inverse, the vee map $(\\cdot)^\\vee : \\mathfrak{so}(3) \\to \\mathbb{R}^3$, extracts the axial vector from any skew-symmetric matrix $S \\in \\mathfrak{so}(3)$ such that $(x^\\wedge)^\\vee = x$ and $(S^\\vee)^\\wedge = S$.\n\n\nProp. 3.1 (Standard $\\mathfrak{so}(3)$ Identities). For all $x, y \\in \\mathbb{R}^3$ and $R \\in SO(3)$:\n\n\n1. $x^\\wedge y = -y^\\wedge x = x \\times y$,\n2. $(x^\\wedge)^2 y = x \\times (x \\times y) = (x \\cdot y) x - |x|^2 y$,\n3. $(R x)^\\wedge = R x^\\wedge R^\\top$ (Adjoint invariance on $SO(3)$),\n4. $\\operatorname{Tr}(x^\\wedge y^\\wedge) = -2, x \\cdot y$,\n5. $\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{Tr}(x^\\wedge S) = -x \\cdot S^\\vee$ for any $S \\in \\mathfrak{so}(3)$.\n\n\n________________\n\n\n3.3 Infinitesimal Variations on $\\mathbb{R}^3$, $\\mathbb{S}^2$, $SO(3)$, and Product Manifolds\nTo linearize nonlinear dynamics along a reference trajectory $q_d(t) \\in Q$ without introducing local coordinates, we perturb the trajectory through a smooth one-parameter family of curves $q^\\epsilon(t) \\in Q$ parameterized by $\\epsilon \\in (-\\epsilon_0, \\epsilon_0)$ such that $q^0(t) = q_d(t)$ (Wu & Sreenath, 2015).\n\n\nDef. 3.2 (Infinitesimal Variation on a Manifold). Given a smooth family of trajectories $q^\\epsilon(t) \\in Q$ with $q^0(t) = q_d(t)$, the infinitesimal variation along $q_d(t)$ is the tangent vector field\n\n\n$$ \\delta q(t) = \\left.\\frac{d}{d\\epsilon}\\right|{\\epsilon=0} q^\\epsilon(t) \\in T{q_d(t)} Q. \\tag{3.2} $$\n3.3.1 Variations on $\\mathbb{R}^3$\nOn $Q = \\mathbb{R}^3$, the tangent space is canonically $T_{x_d} \\mathbb{R}^3 \\cong \\mathbb{R}^3$. A perturbed trajectory $x^\\epsilon(t) = x_d(t) + \\epsilon \\delta x(t) + \\mathcal{O}(\\epsilon^2)$ yields:\n\n\n$$ \\delta x \\in \\mathbb{R}^3, \\qquad \\delta v = \\delta \\dot{x} = \\frac{d}{dt}(\\delta x). \\tag{3.3} $$\n3.3.2 Variations on $\\mathbb{S}^2$\nThe unit sphere $\\mathbb{S}^2 = { q \\in \\mathbb{R}^3 \\mid q \\cdot q = 1 }$ is an embedded submanifold of $\\mathbb{R}^3$ with tangent space $T_{q_d} \\mathbb{S}^2 = { v \\in \\mathbb{R}^3 \\mid v \\cdot q_d = 0 }$. A smooth curve $q^\\epsilon(t) \\in \\mathbb{S}^2$ can be generated via the exponential map on $SO(3)$ acting on $q_d(t)$:\n\n\n$$ q^\\epsilon(t) = \\exp(\\epsilon \\xi^\\wedge(t)), q_d(t), \\qquad \\xi(t) \\cdot q_d(t) = 0. \\tag{3.4} $$\n\n\nDifferentiating Eq. (3.4) with respect to $\\epsilon$ at $\\epsilon = 0$ gives the infinitesimal variation of $q_d$:\n\n\n$$ \\delta q = \\left.\\frac{d}{d\\epsilon}\\right|_{\\epsilon=0} \\exp(\\epsilon \\xi^\\wedge), q_d = \\xi^\\wedge q_d = \\xi \\times q_d, \\qquad \\xi \\cdot q_d = 0. \\tag{3.5} $$\n\n\nThe orthogonality constraint $\\xi \\cdot q_d = 0$ removes the redundant component of $\\xi$ parallel to $q_d$ (which leaves $q_d$ invariant), establishing a bijection between $\\xi \\in T_{q_d} \\mathbb{S}^2$ and $\\delta q \\in T_{q_d} \\mathbb{S}^2$. Differentiating $q^\\epsilon \\cdot q^\\epsilon = 1$ confirms $\\delta q \\cdot q_d = 0$.\n\n\nFor the angular velocity $\\omega^\\epsilon(t) \\in \\mathbb{R}^3$ satisfying $\\dot{q}^\\epsilon = \\omega^\\epsilon \\times q^\\epsilon$ and $\\omega^\\epsilon \\cdot q^\\epsilon = 0$, differentiating $\\omega^\\epsilon \\cdot q^\\epsilon = 0$ with respect to $\\epsilon$ at $\\epsilon = 0$ yields the coupled variation constraint (Wu & Sreenath, 2015):\n\n\n$$ \\delta q \\cdot \\omega_d + q_d \\cdot \\delta\\omega = 0 \\quad \\Longleftrightarrow \\quad (\\xi \\times q_d) \\cdot \\omega_d + q_d \\cdot \\delta\\omega = 0. \\tag{3.6} $$\n\n\nFurthermore, differentiating $\\dot{q}^\\epsilon = \\omega^\\epsilon \\times q^\\epsilon$ with respect to $\\epsilon$ at $\\epsilon = 0$ and noting $\\delta \\dot{q} = \\frac{d}{dt}(\\delta q)$ gives\n\n\n$$ \\delta \\dot{q} = \\dot{\\xi} \\times q_d + \\xi \\times \\dot{q}_d = \\delta\\omega \\times q_d + \\omega_d \\times (\\xi \\times q_d). \\tag{3.7} $$\n3.3.3 Variations on $SO(3)$\nThe special orthogonal group $SO(3) = { R \\in \\mathbb{R}^{3\\times 3} \\mid R^\\top R = I_3,; \\det(R) = 1 }$ has Lie algebra $\\mathfrak{so}(3)$. Using right-trivialized (body-frame) perturbations about $R_d(t) \\in SO(3)$, we write\n\n\n$$ R^\\epsilon(t) = R_d(t) \\exp(\\epsilon \\eta^\\wedge(t)), \\qquad \\eta(t) \\in \\mathbb{R}^3. \\tag{3.8} $$\n\n\nDifferentiating Eq. (3.8) at $\\epsilon = 0$ yields the variation on $SO(3)$:\n\n\n$$ \\delta R = \\left.\\frac{d}{d\\epsilon}\\right|_{\\epsilon=0} R_d \\exp(\\epsilon \\eta^\\wedge) = R_d \\eta^\\wedge. \\tag{3.9} $$\n\n\nTo find the body-frame angular velocity variation $\\delta\\Omega$, recall the kinematic equation $\\dot{R}^\\epsilon = R^\\epsilon (\\Omega^\\epsilon)^\\wedge$, or equivalently $(\\Omega^\\epsilon)^\\wedge = (R^\\epsilon)^\\top \\dot{R}^\\epsilon$. Differentiating $(R^\\epsilon)^\\top \\dot{R}^\\epsilon$ at $\\epsilon = 0$ and using $\\delta(R^\\top) = -\\eta^\\wedge R_d^\\top$ and $\\delta\\dot{R} = \\dot{R}_d \\eta^\\wedge + R_d \\dot{\\eta}^\\wedge$:\n\n\n$$ \\begin{aligned} \\delta \\Omega^\\wedge &= \\delta(R^\\top) \\dot{R}_d + R_d^\\top \\delta\\dot{R} \\ &= -\\eta^\\wedge R_d^\\top (R_d \\Omega_d^\\wedge) + R_d^\\top (R_d \\Omega_d^\\wedge \\eta^\\wedge + R_d \\dot{\\eta}^\\wedge) \\ &= \\Omega_d^\\wedge \\eta^\\wedge - \\eta^\\wedge \\Omega_d^\\wedge + \\dot{\\eta}^\\wedge \\ &= (\\Omega_d^\\wedge \\eta)^\\wedge + \\dot{\\eta}^\\wedge, \\end{aligned} \\tag{3.10} $$\n\n\nwhere we used the Lie bracket identity $[a^\\wedge, b^\\wedge] = a^\\wedge b^\\wedge - b^\\wedge a^\\wedge = (a \\times b)^\\wedge = (a^\\wedge b)^\\wedge$. Applying $(\\cdot)^\\vee$ gives the exact variation of the body angular velocity (Sreenath, Lee, & Kumar, 2013; Wu & Sreenath, 2015):\n\n\n$$ \\delta \\Omega = \\Omega_d^\\wedge \\eta + \\dot{\\eta}. \\tag{3.11} $$\n3.3.4 Variations on Product Manifolds\nConsider a composite system evolving on a product manifold $Q = Q_1 \\times Q_2 \\times \\cdots \\times Q_k$, such as $SE(3) \\cong \\mathbb{R}^3 \\times SO(3)$ for a rigid body, or $Q = \\mathbb{R}^3 \\times SO(3) \\times \\mathbb{S}^2$ for a quadrotor with a taut cable-suspended load (Sreenath, Lee, & Kumar, 2013). Because the tangent space of a product manifold decomposes as $T_{(q_1, \\dots, q_k)} Q \\cong T_{q_1} Q_1 \\oplus \\cdots \\oplus T_{q_k} Q_k$, the infinitesimal variation is simply the tuple of component variations:\n\n\n$$ \\delta q = (\\delta x, \\delta R, \\delta q_{\\text{load}}) = \\big(\\delta x,; R_d \\eta^\\wedge,; \\xi \\times q_d\\big), \\tag{3.12} $$\n\n\nwith state-space variation vector $s = [\\delta x^\\top, \\delta v^\\top, \\eta^\\top, \\delta\\Omega^\\top, \\xi^\\top, \\delta\\omega^\\top]^\\top \\in \\mathbb{R}^{18}$ subject to the linear algebraic constraints $\\xi \\cdot q_d = 0$ and Eq. (3.6) (Wu & Sreenath, 2015).\n\n\n  Tangent space T_{q_d} M           Transport T(q, q_d)          Tangent space T_q M\n  -----------------------        ========================>       --------------------\n  d_2 \\Psi(q, q_d) \\in T*_{q_d} M  <========================  d_1 \\Psi(q, q_d) \\in T*_q M\n                                   -T*(q, q_d) d_1 \\Psi = d_2 \\Psi\n\nFig. 3.1: Duality between the velocity transport map $T(q, q_d) : T_{q_d}Q \\to T_q Q$ and its cotangent pullback $T^(q, q_d) : T_q^* Q \\to T_{q_d}^* Q$ induced by a configuration error function $\\Psi(q, q_d)$.*\n\n\n________________\n\n\n3.4 Configuration Error Functions, Exterior Differentials, and Transport Maps\nTo construct geometric tracking controllers and Control Barrier Functions (CBFs) on manifolds (Wu & Sreenath, 2016), we pair a smooth scalar error function with a compatible velocity transport map.\n\n\nDef. 3.3 (Configuration Error Function). A smooth function $\\Psi : Q \\times Q \\to \\mathbb{R}$ is a configuration error function if it is locally positive definite around the diagonal $\\Delta = {(q, q) \\mid q \\in Q}$:\n\n\n1. $\\Psi(q, q_d) \\ge 0$ for all $q, q_d \\in Q$, with $\\Psi(q, q_d) = 0 \\iff q = q_d$,\n2. $d_1 \\Psi(q_d, q_d) = 0$ and the Hessian of $\\Psi(\\cdot, q_d)$ at $q = q_d$ is positive definite.\n\n\nHere $d_1 \\Psi(q, q_d) \\in T_q^* Q$ and $d_2 \\Psi(q, q_d) \\in T_{q_d}^* Q$ denote the partial exterior differentials of $\\Psi$ with respect to its first and second arguments, defined for all $\\delta q \\in T_q Q$ and $\\delta q_d \\in T_{q_d} Q$ by\n\n\n$$ \\langle d_1 \\Psi(q, q_d),, \\delta q \\rangle = \\left.\\frac{d}{d\\epsilon}\\right|{\\epsilon=0} \\Psi(q^\\epsilon, q_d), \\qquad \\langle d_2 \\Psi(q, q_d),, \\delta q_d \\rangle = \\left.\\frac{d}{d\\epsilon}\\right|{\\epsilon=0} \\Psi(q, q_d^\\epsilon). \\tag{3.13} $$\n\n\nDef. 3.4 (Compatible Transport Map and Velocity Error). A smooth linear map $T(q, q_d) : T_{q_d} Q \\to T_q Q$ is a compatible transport map for $\\Psi$ if its dual (adjoint) $T^(q, q_d) : T_q^ Q \\to T_{q_d}^* Q$ satisfies\n\n\n$$ d_2 \\Psi(q, q_d) = -T^*(q, q_d), d_1 \\Psi(q, q_d). \\tag{3.14} $$\n\n\nGiven $T(q, q_d)$, the velocity error $e_{\\dot{q}} \\in T_q Q$ is defined by transporting $\\dot{q}d \\in T{q_d} Q$ into $T_q Q$ and subtracting:\n\n\n$$ e_{\\dot{q}} = \\dot{q} - T(q, q_d) \\dot{q}_d. \\tag{3.15} $$\n\n\nProp. 3.2 (Energy-Dissipation / Lyapunov Derivative Identity). If $T(q, q_d)$ satisfies the compatibility condition Eq. (3.14), then along any smooth trajectories $q(t), q_d(t) \\in Q$, the time derivative of $\\Psi(q(t), q_d(t))$ depends solely on $d_1 \\Psi$ and the velocity error $e_{\\dot{q}}$:\n\n\n$$ \\begin{aligned} \\dot{\\Psi}(q, q_d) &= \\langle d_1 \\Psi, \\dot{q} \\rangle + \\langle d_2 \\Psi, \\dot{q}_d \\rangle \\ &= \\langle d_1 \\Psi, \\dot{q} \\rangle - \\langle T^*(q, q_d) d_1 \\Psi, \\dot{q}_d \\rangle \\ &= \\langle d_1 \\Psi, \\dot{q} - T(q, q_d) \\dot{q}d \\rangle = \\langle d_1 \\Psi(q, q_d),, e{\\dot{q}} \\rangle. \\end{aligned} \\tag{3.16} $$\n\n\nRemark 3.1. Eq. (3.16) is the geometric foundation of PD and backstepping control on manifolds: it ensures that $\\dot{\\Psi}$ is linear in the velocity error $e_{\\dot{q}}$ without requiring $q$ and $q_d$ to share a tangent space.\n\n\n________________\n\n\n3.4.1 Configuration Error and Transport Map on $SO(3)$\nOn $SO(3)$, the standard trace-based quadratic configuration error function is\n\n\n$$ \\Psi_R(R, R_d) = \\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{Tr}(I_3 - R_d^\\top R). \\tag{3.17} $$\n\n\nLet us compute $d_1 \\Psi_R$ and $d_2 \\Psi_R$ explicitly. Using a body-frame variation $\\delta R = R \\zeta^\\wedge \\in T_R SO(3)$ ($\\zeta \\in \\mathbb{R}^3$):\n\n\n$$ \\begin{aligned} \\langle d_1 \\Psi_R, R\\zeta^\\wedge \\rangle &= -\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{Tr}(R_d^\\top R \\zeta^\\wedge) \\ &= -\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{Tr}\\big((R_d^\\top R - R^\\top R_d) \\zeta^\\wedge\\big) \\ &= \\frac{1}{2}(R_d^\\top R - R^\\top R_d)^\\vee \\cdot \\zeta, \\end{aligned} \\tag{3.18} $$\n\n\nwhere we used $\\operatorname{Tr}(M \\zeta^\\wedge) = \\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{Tr}((M - M^\\top)\\zeta^\\wedge)$ (since the symmetric part of $M$ is orthogonal to $\\zeta^\\wedge$) and Prop. 3.1(5). Identifying $\\mathbb{R}^3 \\cong T_R^* SO(3)$ via body-frame trivialization yields the attitude error vector:\n\n\n$$ e_R = \\frac{1}{2}(R_d^\\top R - R^\\top R_d)^\\vee. \\tag{3.19} $$\n\n\nNext, for a variation $\\delta R_d = R_d \\eta^\\wedge \\in T_{R_d} SO(3)$ ($\\eta \\in \\mathbb{R}^3$), note that $\\delta(R_d^\\top) = -\\eta^\\wedge R_d^\\top$. Thus:\n\n\n$$ \\begin{aligned} \\langle d_2 \\Psi_R, R_d\\eta^\\wedge \\rangle &= \\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{Tr}(\\eta^\\wedge R_d^\\top R) \\ &= \\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{Tr}\\big(\\eta^\\wedge (R_d^\\top R - R^\\top R_d)\\big) \\ &= -\\frac{1}{2}(R_d^\\top R - R^\\top R_d)^\\vee \\cdot \\eta \\ &= -\\big(R^\\top R_d, e_R\\big) \\cdot \\big(R^\\top R_d, \\eta\\big)? \\quad \\text{Wait—let's check the frame!} \\end{aligned} $$\n\n\nCareful! In body-frame trivialization, $\\dot{R} = R\\Omega^\\wedge$ and $\\dot{R}_d = R_d \\Omega_d^\\wedge$. In Eq. (3.18), $d_1 \\Psi_R$ in the body frame of $R$ is $e_R = \\frac{1}{2}(R_d^\\top R - R^\\top R_d)^\\vee$. In the body frame of $R_d$, $d_2 \\Psi_R$ is $-e_R$. Let's check the transport map:\n\n\n$$ T(R, R_d)\\Omega_d = R^\\top R_d \\Omega_d, \\qquad e_\\Omega = \\Omega - R^\\top R_d \\Omega_d. \\tag{3.20} $$\n\n\nWait: how does $T^*(R, R_d)$ act on $e_R$? Notice that $R_d^\\top R$ commutes with $(R_d^\\top R - R^\\top R_d)$! Specifically, by Rodrigues' formula or direct expansion: $(R_d^\\top R) (R_d^\\top R - R^\\top R_d) (R^\\top R_d) = R_d^\\top R - R^\\top R_d$, which implies by Prop. 3.1(3) that\n\n\n$$ (R_d^\\top R), e_R = e_R \\quad \\Longleftrightarrow \\quad (R^\\top R_d), e_R = e_R! \\tag{3.21} $$\n\n\nTherefore, $T^*(R, R_d) e_R = (R^\\top R_d)^\\top e_R = R_d^\\top R, e_R = e_R$, and Eq. (3.14) holds:\n\n\n$$ d_2 \\Psi_R = -e_R = -T^*(R, R_d), d_1 \\Psi_R. \\tag{3.22} $$\n\n\nConsequently, by Prop. 3.2, we immediately obtain the fundamental $SO(3)$ time-derivative identities:\n\n\n$$ \\dot{\\Psi}R = e_R \\cdot e\\Omega, \\qquad \\dot{e}R = C(R_d^\\top R), e\\Omega, \\tag{3.23} $$\n\n\nwhere $C(A) = \\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{Tr}(A) I_3 - A^\\top)$ for $A = R_d^\\top R \\in SO(3)$ (verified in Section 3.6).\n\n\n________________\n\n\n3.4.2 Configuration Error and Transport Map on $\\mathbb{S}^2$\nOn $\\mathbb{S}^2$, the quadratic configuration error function is\n\n\n$$ \\Psi_q(q, q_d) = 1 - q \\cdot q_d. \\tag{3.24} $$\n\n\nFor $\\delta q \\in T_q \\mathbb{S}^2$ ($\\delta q \\cdot q = 0$) and $\\delta q_d \\in T_{q_d} \\mathbb{S}^2$ ($\\delta q_d \\cdot q_d = 0$):\n\n\n$$ \\langle d_1 \\Psi_q, \\delta q \\rangle = -q_d \\cdot \\delta q = \\big(q^\\wedge{}^2 q_d\\big) \\cdot \\delta q, \\qquad \\langle d_2 \\Psi_q, \\delta q_d \\rangle = -q \\cdot \\delta q_d = \\big((q_d^\\wedge)^2 q\\big) \\cdot \\delta q_d, \\tag{3.25} $$\n\n\nwhere we used $(q^\\wedge)^2 q_d = (q \\cdot q_d) q - q_d$ and $(q_d^\\wedge)^2 q = (q_d \\cdot q) q_d - q$. Identifying $T_{q_d}^* \\mathbb{S}^2 \\cong T_{q_d} \\mathbb{S}^2$, we define the $\\mathbb{S}^2$ configuration error vector:\n\n\n$$ e_q = (q_d^\\wedge)^2 q = q_d \\times (q_d \\times q). \\tag{3.26} $$\n\n\nThe compatible transport map $T(q, q_d) : T_{q_d} \\mathbb{S}^2 \\to T_q \\mathbb{S}^2$ and velocity errors are (Sreenath, Lee, & Kumar, 2013; Wu & Sreenath, 2016):\n\n\n$$ T(q, q_d)\\dot{q}_d = (q_d \\times \\dot{q}d) \\times q, \\qquad e{\\dot{q}} = \\dot{q} - (q_d \\times \\dot{q}d) \\times q, \\qquad e\\omega = \\omega - (-q^\\wedge{}^2)\\omega_d. \\tag{3.27} $$\n\n\nProp. 3.3 (Compatibility on $\\mathbb{S}^2$). The transport map $T(q, q_d)$ in Eq. (3.27) satisfies $d_2 \\Psi_q = -T^*(q, q_d) d_1 \\Psi_q$.\n\n\nProof. For any $v \\in T_q \\mathbb{S}^2$ and $w \\in T_{q_d} \\mathbb{S}^2$:\n\n\n$$ \\langle v, T(q, q_d) w \\rangle = v \\cdot ((q_d \\times w) \\times q) = (q \\times v) \\cdot (q_d \\times w) = ((q \\times v) \\times q_d) \\cdot w, $$\n\n\nso $T^*(q, q_d) v = (q \\times v) \\times q_d$. Substituting $v = d_1 \\Psi_q = q \\times (q \\times q_d) = (q \\cdot q_d)q - q_d$, we have $q \\times v = -q \\times q_d = q_d \\times q$, and hence\n\n\n$$ -T^*(q, q_d) d_1 \\Psi_q = -((q_d \\times q) \\times q_d) = q_d \\times (q_d \\times q) = (q_d^\\wedge)^2 q = d_2 \\Psi_q = e_q. \\quad \\blacksquare $$\n\n\nManifold $Q$\n\tConfig. Error $\\Psi(q, q_d)$\n\tError Vector\n\tTransport Map $T(q, q_d) \\dot{q}_d$\n\tVelocity Error\n\t$\\mathbb{R}^3$\n\t$\\frac{1}{2}|x - x_d|^2$\n\t$e_x = x - x_d$\n\t$\\dot{x}_d$\n\t$e_v = \\dot{x} - \\dot{x}_d$\n\t$\\mathbb{S}^2$\n\t$1 - q \\cdot q_d$\n\t$e_q = (q_d^\\wedge)^2 q$\n\t$(q_d \\times \\dot{q}_d) \\times q$\n\t$e_{\\dot{q}} = \\dot{q} - (q_d \\times \\dot{q}d) \\times q$, $e\\omega = \\omega - (-q^\\wedge{}^2)\\omega_d$\n\t$SO(3)$\n\t$\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{Tr}(I_3 - R_d^\\top R)$\n\t$e_R = \\frac{1}{2}(R_d^\\top R - R^\\top R_d)^\\vee$\n\t$R^\\top R_d \\Omega_d$\n\t$e_\\Omega = \\Omega - R^\\top R_d \\Omega_d$\n\t\n\nTable 3.1: Summary of quadratic configuration error functions, error vectors, compatible transport maps, and velocity errors across $\\mathbb{R}^3$, $\\mathbb{S}^2$, and $SO(3)$.\n\n\n________________\n\n\n3.5 First-Order Equivalence Between Tracking Errors and Variations\nA central bridge between nonlinear geometric control (Sreenath, Lee, & Kumar, 2013) and variation-based linearization (Wu & Sreenath, 2015) is that, to first order in $\\epsilon$, the nonlinear tracking errors $(e_R, e_\\Omega)$ and $(e_q, e_\\omega)$ coincide with the infinitesimal variations $(\\eta, \\delta\\Omega)$ and $(\\xi, \\delta\\omega)$ up to known signs/projections.\n3.5.1 Equivalence on $SO(3)$: $(\\eta, \\delta\\Omega) \\leftrightarrow (e_R, e_\\Omega)$\nLet $R^\\epsilon = R_d \\exp(\\epsilon \\eta^\\wedge) = R_d (I_3 + \\epsilon \\eta^\\wedge) + \\mathcal{O}(\\epsilon^2)$ and $\\Omega^\\epsilon = \\Omega_d + \\epsilon \\delta\\Omega + \\mathcal{O}(\\epsilon^2)$.\n\n\n1. Attitude error:\n\n\n$$ R_d^\\top R^\\epsilon = I_3 + \\epsilon \\eta^\\wedge + \\mathcal{O}(\\epsilon^2) \\implies e_R^\\epsilon = \\frac{1}{2}\\big((I_3 + \\epsilon\\eta^\\wedge) - (I_3 - \\epsilon\\eta^\\wedge)\\big)^\\vee + \\mathcal{O}(\\epsilon^2) = \\epsilon \\eta + \\mathcal{O}(\\epsilon^2). \\tag{3.28} $$\n\n\n2. Angular velocity error: Using $(R^\\epsilon)^\\top R_d = I_3 - \\epsilon \\eta^\\wedge + \\mathcal{O}(\\epsilon^2)$ and Eq. (3.11) ($\\delta\\Omega = \\Omega_d^\\wedge \\eta + \\dot{\\eta}$):\n\n\n$$ \\begin{aligned} e_\\Omega^\\epsilon &= \\Omega^\\epsilon - (R^\\epsilon)^\\top R_d \\Omega_d \\ &= (\\Omega_d + \\epsilon \\delta\\Omega) - (I_3 - \\epsilon \\eta^\\wedge)\\Omega_d + \\mathcal{O}(\\epsilon^2) \\ &= \\epsilon (\\delta\\Omega + \\eta^\\wedge \\Omega_d) + \\mathcal{O}(\\epsilon^2) \\ &= \\epsilon (\\Omega_d^\\wedge \\eta + \\dot{\\eta} - \\Omega_d^\\wedge \\eta) + \\mathcal{O}(\\epsilon^2) = \\epsilon \\dot{\\eta} + \\mathcal{O}(\\epsilon^2). \\end{aligned} \\tag{3.29} $$\n\n\nThus, $\\lim_{\\epsilon \\to 0} \\frac{e_R^\\epsilon}{\\epsilon} = \\eta$ and $\\lim_{\\epsilon \\to 0} \\frac{e_\\Omega^\\epsilon}{\\epsilon} = \\dot{\\eta} = \\delta\\Omega - \\Omega_d^\\wedge \\eta$ (Wu & Sreenath, 2015).\n3.5.2 Equivalence on $\\mathbb{S}^2$: $(\\xi, \\delta\\omega) \\leftrightarrow (e_q, e_\\omega)$\nLet $q^\\epsilon = \\exp(\\epsilon \\xi^\\wedge) q_d = q_d + \\epsilon (\\xi \\times q_d) + \\mathcal{O}(\\epsilon^2)$ with $\\xi \\cdot q_d = 0$, and $\\omega^\\epsilon = \\omega_d + \\epsilon \\delta\\omega + \\mathcal{O}(\\epsilon^2)$ with $\\omega_d \\cdot q_d = 0$.\n\n\n1. Configuration error: Since $(q_d^\\wedge)^2 q_d = 0$ and $(q_d^\\wedge)^2 (\\xi \\times q_d) = q_d \\times (q_d \\times (\\xi \\times q_d)) = q_d \\times \\xi = -(\\xi \\times q_d)$ (because $\\xi \\cdot q_d = 0$):\n\n\n$$ e_q^\\epsilon = (q_d^\\wedge)^2 q^\\epsilon = \\epsilon (q_d^\\wedge)^2 (\\xi \\times q_d) + \\mathcal{O}(\\epsilon^2) = -\\epsilon (\\xi \\times q_d) + \\mathcal{O}(\\epsilon^2) = -\\epsilon \\delta q + \\mathcal{O}(\\epsilon^2). \\tag{3.30} $$\n\n\n2. Angular velocity error: Expanding $-(q^\\epsilon)^\\wedge{}^2 \\omega_d = (I_3 - q^\\epsilon (q^\\epsilon)^\\top) \\omega_d$ using $\\omega_d \\cdot q_d = 0$:\n\n\n$$ \\begin{aligned} -(q^\\epsilon)^\\wedge{}^2 \\omega_d &= \\omega_d - \\big(q_d + \\epsilon \\delta q\\big)\\big((q_d + \\epsilon \\delta q) \\cdot \\omega_d\\big) + \\mathcal{O}(\\epsilon^2) \\ &= \\omega_d - \\epsilon (\\delta q \\cdot \\omega_d) q_d + \\mathcal{O}(\\epsilon^2). \\end{aligned} $$\n\n\nSubstituting into $e_\\omega^\\epsilon = \\omega^\\epsilon - (-(q^\\epsilon)^\\wedge{}^2 \\omega_d)$ and applying Eq. (3.6) ($(\\delta q \\cdot \\omega_d) + q_d \\cdot \\delta\\omega = 0$):\n\n\n$$ e_\\omega^\\epsilon = \\epsilon \\big(\\delta\\omega + (\\delta q \\cdot \\omega_d) q_d\\big) + \\mathcal{O}(\\epsilon^2) = \\epsilon \\big(\\delta\\omega - (q_d \\cdot \\delta\\omega) q_d\\big) + \\mathcal{O}(\\epsilon^2) = \\epsilon (-q_d^\\wedge{}^2 \\delta\\omega) + \\mathcal{O}(\\epsilon^2). \\tag{3.31} $$\n\n\nThat is, $e_\\omega^\\epsilon/\\epsilon$ converges to the projection of $\\delta\\omega$ onto $T_{q_d}\\mathbb{S}^2$!\n\n\n________________\n\n\n3.6 Worked Derivations\nWorked Derivation 3.1: Proof of $\\dot{e}R = C(R_d^\\top R) e\\Omega$\nLet $A = R_d^\\top R \\in SO(3)$. Using $\\dot{R} = R\\Omega^\\wedge$ and $\\dot{R}_d = R_d \\Omega_d^\\wedge$:\n\n\n$$ \\dot{A} = -\\Omega_d^\\wedge R_d^\\top R + R_d^\\top R \\Omega^\\wedge = A \\big(\\Omega^\\wedge - A^\\top \\Omega_d^\\wedge A\\big) = A \\big(\\Omega - R^\\top R_d \\Omega_d\\big)^\\wedge = A e_\\Omega^\\wedge. $$\n\n\nDifferentiating $e_R = \\frac{1}{2}(A - A^\\top)^\\vee$ gives\n\n\n$$ \\dot{e}R = \\frac{1}{2}(\\dot{A} - \\dot{A}^\\top)^\\vee = \\frac{1}{2}\\big(A e\\Omega^\\wedge + e_\\Omega^\\wedge A^\\top\\big)^\\vee. $$\n\n\nFor any $A \\in SO(3)$ and $x \\in \\mathbb{R}^3$, expanding the $(i, j)$ entry via the Levi-Civita symbol or testing on $A = \\exp(\\theta u^\\wedge)$ yields $\\frac{1}{2}(A x^\\wedge + x^\\wedge A^\\top)^\\vee = \\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{Tr}(A) I_3 - A^\\top) x$. Setting $x = e_\\Omega$ gives $\\dot{e}R = C(R_d^\\top R) e\\Omega$ with $C(A) = \\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{Tr}(A) I_3 - A^\\top)$, which is symmetric-positive-definite whenever $\\Psi_R < 2$ (and satisfies $|C(A)|_2 \\le 1$) (Sreenath, Lee, & Kumar, 2013; Kotaru, Edmonson, & Sreenath, 2020).\nWorked Derivation 3.2: Transport of Adaptive Uncertainty on $SO(3)$\nIn $\\mathcal{L}1$ adaptive geometric attitude control (Kotaru, Edmonson, & Sreenath, 2020), the true attitude error dynamics are written in terms of $(e_R, e\\Omega)$ with matched uncertainty $\\theta \\in \\mathbb{R}^3$ and virtual control $\\mu \\in \\mathbb{R}^3$. Because the reference model tracks $(\\hat{R}, \\hat{\\Omega})$ while the true system is at $(R, \\Omega)$, comparing $\\hat{\\Omega}$ and $\\Omega$ requires the same transport map $T(R, \\hat{R})\\hat{\\Omega} = R^\\top \\hat{R} \\hat{\\Omega}$, yielding the prediction error $\\tilde{e}_\\Omega = \\Omega - R^\\top \\hat{R}\\hat{\\Omega}$.\n\n\n________________\n\n\n3.7 Exercises\n1. Exercise 3.1 ($\\mathfrak{so}(3)$ Bracket Identity). Prove directly from Eq. (3.1) that for any $a, b \\in \\mathbb{R}^3$, $a^\\wedge b^\\wedge - b^\\wedge a^\\wedge = (a \\times b)^\\wedge$.\n2. Exercise 3.2 (Bounds on $|e_R|$). Show that $|e_R|^2 = (2 - \\Psi_R)\\Psi_R$ for $\\Psi_R = \\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{Tr}(I_3 - R_d^\\top R)$, and deduce that $|e_R| \\le 1$ globally on $SO(3)$.\n3. Exercise 3.3 (Bounds on $|e_q|$). Show that $|e_q|^2 = (2 - \\Psi_q)\\Psi_q$ for $\\Psi_q = 1 - q \\cdot q_d$ on $\\mathbb{S}^2$.\n4. Exercise 3.4 (Variation of $\\dot{q}$ on $\\mathbb{S}^2$). Starting from Eq. (3.7), express $\\delta\\omega$ in terms of $\\xi, \\dot{\\xi}, q_d, \\omega_d$ when $\\omega_d \\cdot q_d = 0$ and $\\delta\\omega \\cdot q_d = -(\\xi \\times q_d)\\cdot \\omega_d$.\n5. Exercise 3.5 (Product Manifold Transport). Write out the full configuration error $\\Psi(X, X_d)$, compatible transport map $T(X, X_d)$, and velocity error $e_{\\dot{X}}$ for a quadrotor with a cable-suspended load evolving on $Q = \\mathbb{R}^3 \\times SO(3) \\times \\mathbb{S}^2$.\n\n\n________________\n\n\nReferences\n1. Wu, G., & Sreenath, K. (2015). Variation-Based Linearization of Nonlinear Systems Evolving on $SO(3)$ and $\\mathbb{S}^2$. IEEE Access. PDF\n2. Wu, G., & Sreenath, K. (2016). Safety-Critical Geometric Control for Systems on Manifolds Subject to Time-Varying Constraints. IEEE Transactions on Automatic Control. PDF\n3. Sreenath, K., Lee, T., & Kumar, V. (2013). Geometric Control and Differential Flatness of a Quadrotor UAV with a Cable-Suspended Load. IEEE CDC. PDF\n4. Kotaru, P., Edmonson, R., & Sreenath, K. (2020). Geometric $L_1$ Adaptive Attitude Control for a Quadrotor UAV. ASME JDSMC. PDF\n\n```mermaid\nflowchart LR\n  QD[\"T_{q_d} M\"] -->|\"Transport Map T(q, q_d)\"| Q[\"T_q M\"]\n  Q -->|\"Error e_qdot = qdot - T qdot_d\"| L[\"Lyapunov Descent\"]\n```\n"}, {"id": "ch04", "part": "Part II: Modeling", "label": "Ch 4. Coordinate-Free Dynamics", "verified": true, "chapterFile": "ch04_coordinate_free_dynamics.md", "notebook": "ch04_lagrange_dalembert_sim.ipynb", "skill": "ch04-manifold-dynamics-modeling", "diagramKey": "lagrange_dalembert_variation", "markdown": "﻿Chapter 4: Coordinate-Free Dynamics: Riemannian Mechanics, Lagrange–d'Alembert & Multi-Body Formulations\nChapter Roadmap. Classical Euler–Lagrange equations written in local coordinates $q \\in \\mathbb{R}^n$ suffer from chart singularities (such as gimbal lock on $SO(3)$ or polar singularities on $S^2$) and obscure the geometric structure of mechanical energy and constraints. This chapter develops coordinate-free equations of motion directly on configuration manifolds: (1) Riemannian simple mechanical systems via the Levi-Civita covariant derivative, (2) the Lagrange–d'Alembert principle on $SE(3) \\times S^2$ for a quadrotor with a cable-suspended load (including taut/slack hybrid transitions), (3) Newton–Euler flexible-cable chains on $SO(3) \\times \\mathbb{R}^3 \\times (S^2)^n$ and modular Boltzmann–Hamel whole-body dynamics on $SE(3) \\times \\mathbb{R}^{N_T}$, and (4) worked examples and exercises.\n\n\n________________\n\n\n4.1 Notation and Lie-Algebraic Conventions\nThroughout this chapter, we adhere to the following canonical conventions:\n\n\n* Hat and Vee Maps. The hat map $(\\cdot)^\\wedge : \\mathbb{R}^3 \\to \\mathfrak{so}(3)$ is the Lie algebra isomorphism defined by $$x^\\wedge y = x \\times y, \\qquad \\forall x, y \\in \\mathbb{R}^3, \\tag{4.1}$$ explicitly given for $x = [x_1, x_2, x_3]^T$ by $$x^\\wedge = \\begin{bmatrix} 0 & -x_3 & x_2 \\ x_3 & 0 & -x_1 \\ -x_2 & x_1 & 0 \\end{bmatrix}, \\tag{4.2}$$ with inverse $(\\cdot)^\\vee : \\mathfrak{so}(3) \\to \\mathbb{R}^3$.\n* Kinematics on $SO(3)$. For a rigid body with attitude $R \\in SO(3) := {R \\in \\mathbb{R}^{3\\times 3} \\mid R^T R = I,, \\det(R) = +1}$, the body-fixed angular velocity $\\Omega \\in \\mathbb{R}^3$ satisfies $$\\dot{R} = R \\Omega^\\wedge. \\tag{4.3}$$\n* Kinematics on $S^2$. For a unit direction vector $q \\in S^2 := {q \\in \\mathbb{R}^3 \\mid q \\cdot q = 1}$, its angular velocity $\\omega \\in \\mathbb{R}^3$ satisfies $$\\dot{q} = \\omega \\times q = \\omega^\\wedge q, \\qquad \\omega \\cdot q = 0. \\tag{4.4}$$ The orthogonality condition $\\omega \\cdot q = 0$ ensures uniqueness of $\\omega$ in the tangent plane $T_q S^2 \\cong {v \\in \\mathbb{R}^3 \\mid v \\cdot q = 0}$.\n\n\nSymbol\n\tSpace\n\tPhysical Meaning\n\t$M$, $T_q M$, $T_q^* M$\n\tManifold, tangent, cotangent\n\tConfiguration space and dual velocity/force fibers\n\t$\\llangle \\cdot, \\cdot \\rrangle_q$\n\t$T_q M \\times T_q M \\to \\mathbb{R}$\n\tInertia Riemannian metric $\\langle M_q \\dot{q}_1, \\dot{q}_2 \\rangle$\n\t$\\nabla_X Y$\n\t$\\Gamma(TM)$\n\tLevi-Civita covariant derivative compatible with $M_q$\n\t$R, \\Omega$\n\t$SO(3), \\mathbb{R}^3$\n\tQuadrotor/base attitude and body angular velocity\n\t$q, \\omega$\n\t$S^2, \\mathbb{R}^3$\n\tCable/pendulum unit direction and angular velocity ($\\omega \\cdot q = 0$)\n\t$x_Q, v_Q, x_L, v_L$\n\t$\\mathbb{R}^3$\n\tQuadrotor and load inertial positions and velocities\n\t$f, M$\n\t$\\mathbb{R}, \\mathbb{R}^3$\n\tQuadrotor scalar thrust and body-frame moment\n\t$g_b, \\theta$\n\t$SE(3), \\mathbb{R}^{N_T}$\n\tFloating-base pose and $N_T$ internal joint angles\n\t\n\nTable 4.1: Summary of coordinate-free geometric and mechanical notation used in Chapter 4.\n\n\n________________\n\n\n4.2 Riemannian Simple Mechanical Systems\nFollowing the geometric control formulation in Wu & Sreenath (IEEE TAC 2016), a mechanical system evolving on a smooth $m$-dimensional configuration manifold $M$ is modeled directly on the tangent bundle $TM = \\bigcup_{q \\in M} T_q M$ without choosing local charts.\n\n\nDef. 4.1 (Simple Mechanical System on a Riemannian Manifold). A simple mechanical system is a tuple $(M, M_q, V_q, {F_i}_{i=1}^m)$ consisting of:\n\n\n1. A smooth configuration manifold $M$ with state $(q, \\dot{q}) \\in TM$.\n2. An inertia tensor $M_q : T_q M \\to T_q^* M$ that induces a Riemannian metric (the kinetic-energy metric): $$\\llangle \\dot{q}_1, \\dot{q}_2 \\rrangle_q = \\langle M_q \\dot{q}_1, \\dot{q}_2 \\rangle, \\qquad \\forall \\dot{q}_1, \\dot{q}_2 \\in T_q M, \\tag{4.5}$$ so that the kinetic energy is $T(q, \\dot{q}) = \\frac{1}{2} \\llangle \\dot{q}, \\dot{q} \\rrangle_q$.\n3. The unique torsion-free, metric-compatible Levi-Civita connection $\\nabla$ determined by $\\llangle \\cdot, \\cdot \\rrangle_q$, which defines the geometric acceleration $\\nabla_{\\dot{q}} \\dot{q} \\in T_q M$.\n4. A smooth potential energy function $V_q : M \\to \\mathbb{R}$ whose differential $dV_q(q) \\in T_q^* M$ is a one-form acting on $X \\in T_q M$ via $\\langle dV_q, X \\rangle_q = \\left.\\frac{d}{dt}\\right|_{t=0} V_q(\\alpha(t))$ for a curve $\\alpha(0)=q, \\dot{\\alpha}(0)=X$.\n5. A collection of input force one-forms $F_i(q, \\dot{q}) \\in T_q^* M$ ($i = 1, \\dots, m$) paired with scalar control inputs $u_i \\in \\mathbb{R}$.\n\n\nProp. 4.1 (Coordinate-Free Equations of Motion). The dynamics of a simple mechanical system $(M, M_q, V_q, {F_i})$ are governed by the second-order geometric differential equation $$\\nabla_{\\dot{q}} \\dot{q} = M_q^{-1}!\\left(-dV_q(q) + \\sum_{i=1}^m F_i(q, \\dot{q}) u_i\\right). \\tag{4.6}$$\n\n\nRemark 4.1 (Why Covariant Acceleration Matters). In Euclidean space $M = \\mathbb{R}^n$ with constant mass $M_q = m I$, the Christoffel symbols vanish and $\\nabla_{\\dot{q}} \\dot{q} = \\ddot{q}$. On curved manifolds such as $S^2$ or $SO(3)$, $\\nabla_{\\dot{q}} \\dot{q}$ automatically incorporates the centripetal/Coriolis curvature terms needed to keep the trajectory tangent to $M$. For example, on $S^2$ with the standard round metric $\\llangle \\dot{q}_1, \\dot{q}_2 \\rrangle = \\dot{q}_1 \\cdot \\dot{q}2$, the covariant acceleration of a curve $q(t) \\in S^2$ is the orthogonal projection of $\\ddot{q}$ onto $T_q S^2$: $$\\nabla{\\dot{q}} \\dot{q} = \\ddot{q} + (\\dot{q} \\cdot \\dot{q}) q = -q^\\wedge q^\\wedge \\ddot{q}. \\tag{4.7}$$\n4.2.1 Configuration Errors and Transport Maps\nTo compare velocities at a current configuration $q \\in M$ and a reference configuration $q_d \\in M$—which live in different tangent spaces $T_q M$ and $T_{q_d} M$—we pair a smooth quadratic configuration error function $\\Psi : M \\times M \\to [0, \\infty)$ with a compatible transport map $T_{(q, q_d)} : T_{q_d} M \\to T_q M$ (Wu & Sreenath, IEEE TAC 2016): $$e_q = d_1 \\Psi(q, q_d), \\qquad d_2 \\Psi(q, q_d) = -T_{(q, q_d)}^* d_1 \\Psi(q, q_d), \\qquad e_{\\dot{q}} = \\dot{q} - T_{(q, q_d)} \\dot{q}_d. \\tag{4.8}$$\n\n\nManifold $M$\n\tError Function $\\Psi(q, q_d)$\n\tConfiguration Error $e_q = d_1\\Psi$\n\tVelocity Error $e_{\\dot{q}}$\n\t$\\mathbb{R}^3$\n\t$\\frac{1}{2}|q - q_d|^2$\n\t$q - q_d$\n\t$\\dot{q} - \\dot{q}_d$\n\t$S^2$\n\t$1 - q \\cdot q_d$\n\t$(q_d \\times q)$ (or $q^\\wedge q^\\wedge q_d$ in $T_q S^2$)\n\t$\\dot{q} - (q_d \\times \\dot{q}_d) \\times q$\n\t$SO(3)$\n\t$\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{tr}(I - R_d^T R)$\n\t$\\frac{1}{2}(R_d^T R - R^T R_d)^\\vee$\n\t$\\Omega - R^T R_d \\Omega_d$\n\t\n\nTable 4.2: Canonical configuration errors and compatible transport maps on $\\mathbb{R}^3$, $S^2$, and $SO(3)$.\n\n\n________________\n\n\n4.3 Lagrange–d'Alembert Principle on $SE(3) \\times S^2$\nWe now derive the coordinate-free dynamics of a quadrotor UAV carrying a point-mass payload via a massless cable, following Sreenath, Lee, & Kumar (CDC 2013).\n4.3.1 Configuration Space and Kinematics (Taut Mode)\nWhen the cable of length $l > 0$ is taut, the system has $8$ degrees of freedom and evolves on the product manifold $$Q_n = SE(3) \\times S^2 \\cong \\mathbb{R}^3 \\times SO(3) \\times S^2, \\tag{4.9}$$ with $4$ control inputs $(f, M) \\in \\mathbb{R} \\times \\mathbb{R}^3$ ($4$ degrees of underactuation). Using the $z$-up inertial frame ($e_3 = [0, 0, 1]^T$ pointing upward) and letting $q \\in S^2$ denote the unit vector from the quadrotor center of mass $x_Q \\in \\mathbb{R}^3$ to the load position $x_L \\in \\mathbb{R}^3$, the kinematic constraint is $$x_Q = x_L - l q, \\qquad v_Q = v_L - l \\dot{q}. \\tag{4.10}$$\n\n\nThe kinetic energy $T : TQ_n \\to \\mathbb{R}$ and potential energy $U : Q_n \\to \\mathbb{R}$ are $$T = \\frac{1}{2} m_Q v_Q \\cdot v_Q + \\frac{1}{2} m_L v_L \\cdot v_L + \\frac{1}{2} \\langle \\Omega, J_Q \\Omega \\rangle, \\tag{4.11}$$ $$U = m_Q g e_3 \\cdot x_Q + m_L g e_3 \\cdot x_L, \\tag{4.12}$$ with Lagrangian $L = T - U$. Here $m_Q, m_L > 0$ are the quadrotor and load masses, and $J_Q \\in \\mathbb{R}^{3\\times 3}$ is the symmetric positive-definite quadrotor inertia tensor in the body frame.\n4.3.2 Manifold Variations and the Lagrange–d'Alembert Principle\nTo take variations directly on $SE(3) \\times S^2$ without local coordinates, we define infinitesimal variations parameterized by $(\\delta x_L, \\eta, \\xi)$ where $\\eta \\in \\mathbb{R}^3$ and $\\xi \\in \\mathbb{R}^3$ with $\\xi \\cdot q = 0$: $$\\delta R = R \\hat{\\eta}, \\qquad \\delta \\Omega = \\hat{\\Omega}\\eta + \\dot{\\eta}, \\tag{4.13}$$ $$\\delta q = \\xi \\times q, \\qquad \\delta \\dot{q} = \\dot{\\xi} \\times q + \\xi \\times \\dot{q}, \\qquad \\delta x_Q = \\delta x_L - l \\delta q. \\tag{4.14}$$ All variations vanish at the endpoints $t = 0$ and $t = \\tau$.\n\n\nDef. 4.2 (Lagrange–d'Alembert Principle). In the presence of non-conservative actuator forces $(f, M)$, the physical trajectory on $[0, \\tau]$ satisfies $$\\delta \\int_0^\\tau L , dt + \\int_0^\\tau \\left( \\langle W_1, \\hat{M} \\rangle_{\\mathfrak{so}(3)} + W_2 \\cdot f R e_3 \\right) dt = 0, \\tag{4.15}$$ where $W_1 = R^T \\delta R = \\hat{\\eta}$ and $W_2 = \\delta x_Q = \\delta x_L - l \\delta q$ are the virtual displacement vector fields.\n4.3.3 Equations of Motion in Taut Mode ($\\Sigma_n$)\nEvaluating Eq. (4.15) using Eqs. (4.13)–(4.14), integrating by parts, and applying the fundamental lemma of the calculus of variations yields the coordinate-free equations of motion on $SE(3) \\times S^2$ (Sreenath, Lee, & Kumar, CDC 2013):\n\n\n$$\\dot{x}_L = v_L, \\tag{4.16}$$ $$(m_Q + m_L)(\\dot{v}_L + g e_3) = \\big(q \\cdot f R e_3 - m_Q l (\\dot{q} \\cdot \\dot{q})\\big) q, \\tag{4.17}$$ $$\\dot{q} = \\omega \\times q, \\qquad (\\omega \\cdot q = 0), \\tag{4.18}$$ $$m_Q l \\dot{\\omega} = -q \\times f R e_3, \\tag{4.19}$$ $$\\dot{R} = R \\Omega^\\wedge, \\tag{4.20}$$ $$J_Q \\dot{\\Omega} + \\Omega \\times J_Q \\Omega = M. \\tag{4.21}$$\n\n\nRemark 4.2 (Decoupling and Second-Order Form on $S^2$). Notice from Eq. (4.19) that gravity does not directly enter the cable attitude dynamics $\\dot{\\omega}$, and the quadrotor attitude dynamics Eq. (4.21) are decoupled from the load. Differentiating Eq. (4.18) and substituting Eq. (4.19) gives the equivalent second-order form on $S^2$: $$m_Q l \\ddot{q} + m_Q l (\\dot{q} \\cdot \\dot{q}) q = q \\times (q \\times f R e_3) = -(I - q q^T) f R e_3, \\tag{4.22}$$ which matches the Riemannian covariant acceleration $\\nabla_{\\dot{q}}\\dot{q}$ in Eq. (4.7) driven by the tangential projection of the thrust vector.\n4.3.4 Hybrid Dynamics: Taut ($\\Sigma_n$) vs. Slack ($\\Sigma_z$) Modes\nWhen the cable tension $T := |m_L (\\dot{v}_L + g e_3)|$ drops to zero, the cable goes slack and the system transitions to $Q_z = SE(3) \\times \\mathbb{R}^3$, where the quadrotor and load evolve as decoupled rigid-body and free-fall point-mass systems (Sreenath, Lee, & Kumar, CDC 2013): $$\\dot{x}_L = v_L, \\qquad m_L(\\dot{v}_L + g e_3) = 0, \\tag{4.23}$$ $$\\dot{x}_Q = v_Q, \\qquad m_Q(\\dot{v}_Q + g e_3) = f R e_3, \\tag{4.24}$$ $$\\dot{R} = R \\Omega^\\wedge, \\qquad J_Q \\dot{\\Omega} + \\Omega \\times J_Q \\Omega = M. \\tag{4.25}$$\n\n\n       +---------------------------------------------------+\n       |  Taut Mode Sigma_n on SE(3) x S^2                 |\n       |  State: X_n = (x_L, v_L, q, omega, R, Omega)      |\n       +---------------------------------------------------+\n          |                                             ^\nGuard S_z: T == 0                           Guard S_n: ||x_Q - x_L|| == l\nDelta_{n->z} = id                           and d/dt ||x_Q - x_L|| > 0\n          v                                 Delta_{z->n}: inelastic impact\n       +---------------------------------------------------+\n       |  Slack Mode Sigma_z on SE(3) x R^3                |\n       |  State: X_z = (x_L, v_L, x_Q, v_Q, R, Omega)      |\n       +---------------------------------------------------+\n\nFig. 4.1: Hybrid automaton switching between the taut cable mode $\\Sigma_n$ on $SE(3) \\times S^2$ and the slack free-fall mode $\\Sigma_z$ on $SE(3) \\times \\mathbb{R}^3$.\n\n\n________________\n\n\n4.4 Multi-Body Formulations\n4.4.1 Flexible-Cable Chains on $SO(3) \\times \\mathbb{R}^3 \\times (S^2)^n$\nWhen cable mass and deformation cannot be neglected, Kotaru, Wu, & Sreenath (arXiv:1711.04895) model the flexible cable as a finite-element chain of $n$ massless rigid links of lengths $l_i$ connected by spherical joints $q_i \\in S^2$, with lumped masses $m_i$ at the end of each link ($m_n = m_L$). The configuration space is $$Q_{\\text{flex}} = SO(3) \\times \\mathbb{R}^3 \\times (S^2)^n, \\tag{4.26}$$ having $6 + 2n$ degrees of freedom and $2n + 2$ degrees of underactuation. The link positions satisfy the kinematic chain $$x_i = x_{i-1} + l_i q_i, \\qquad i \\in {1, \\dots, n}, \\tag{4.27}$$ with $x_0 = x_Q$ and $x_n = x_L$. Applying the Newton–Euler method with internal cable tensions $T_i \\in \\mathbb{R}$ yields $$m_Q(\\ddot{x}0 + g e_3) = f R e_3 + T_1 q_1, \\tag{4.28}$$ $$m_j(\\ddot{x}j + g e_3) = -T_j q_j + T{j+1} q{j+1}, \\qquad j \\in {1, \\dots, n-1}, \\tag{4.29}$$ $$m_n(\\ddot{x}n + g e_3) = -T_n q_n, \\tag{4.30}$$ $$J_Q \\dot{\\Omega} + \\Omega \\times J_Q \\Omega = M. \\tag{4.31}$$ Eliminating the internal tensions $T_1, \\dots, T_n$ by projecting onto the tangent spaces $T{q_i} S^2$ produces a coupled block-matrix system in $(\\ddot{x}_0, \\ddot{q}_1, \\dots, \\ddot{q}n)$ directly on $Q{\\text{flex}}$, which remains differentially flat with flat outputs $Y = (x_n, \\psi)$ requiring $(2n + 4)$ derivatives of $x_n$.\n4.4.2 Modular Boltzmann–Hamel Dynamics on $SE(3) \\times \\mathbb{R}^{N_T}$\nFor floating-base multi-legged robots with $N$ limbs and $N_T = \\sum_{i=1}^N n_i$ internal joints, the configuration space is $SE(3) \\times \\mathbb{R}^{N_T}$ with base pose $g_b \\in SE(3)$, body twist $\\xi_b = [v_b^T, \\Omega_b^T]^T \\in \\mathbb{R}^6$, and joint angles $\\theta \\in \\mathbb{R}^{N_T}$. Using the screw-theoretic framework of Fast & Modular Whole-Body Lagrangian Dynamics (arXiv:2504.16383), we define the $SE(3)$ Adjoint and Lie-bracket operators: $$\\mathbf{Ad}{g{a,b}} := \\begin{bmatrix} R_{a,b} & P_{a,b}^\\wedge R_{a,b} \\ \\mathbf{0}{3\\times 3} & R{a,b} \\end{bmatrix}, \\qquad \\mathbf{ad}\\xi := \\begin{bmatrix} \\omega^\\wedge & v^\\wedge \\ \\mathbf{0}{3\\times 3} & \\omega^\\wedge \\end{bmatrix}. \\tag{4.32}$$\n\n\nProp. 4.2 (Modular Boltzmann–Hamel Equations). The whole-body dynamics on $SE(3) \\times \\mathbb{R}^{N_T}$ take the singularity-free quasi-coordinate form $$M(\\theta) \\begin{bmatrix} \\dot{\\xi}b \\ \\ddot{\\theta} \\end{bmatrix} + C(\\theta, \\xi_b, \\dot{\\theta}) \\begin{bmatrix} \\xi_b \\ \\dot{\\theta} \\end{bmatrix} + G(g_b, \\theta) = \\begin{bmatrix} \\mathbf{0}{6\\times 1} \\ \\tau \\end{bmatrix} + \\mathcal{J}^T F_{\\text{ext}}, \\tag{4.33}$$ where the mass matrix decomposes additively across individual link modules $(i, j)$: $$M(\\theta) = \\begin{bmatrix} I_b & \\mathbf{0}{6\\times N_T} \\ \\mathbf{0}{N_T\\times 6} & \\mathbf{0}{N_T\\times N_T} \\end{bmatrix} + \\sum{i=1}^N \\sum_{j=1}^{n_i} M_{ij}(\\theta), \\tag{4.34}$$ and the contact Jacobian $\\mathcal{J} \\in \\mathbb{R}^{6N \\times (6+N_T)}$ has block rows $[\\mathbf{Ad}{g{b,it}}^{-1},, \\mathbf{Ad}{g{b,it}}^{-1} J_{it}]$.\n\n\nBecause every link $(i,j)$ contributes additively to $M(\\theta)$ and $C(\\theta, \\xi_b, \\dot{\\theta})$, morphological changes—such as a locked joint or lost limb—are handled by matrix addition, subtraction, and reshaping without re-deriving symbolic derivatives.\n\n\n________________\n\n\n4.5 Worked Examples\nExample 4.1: Unforced Conservation Laws on $SE(3) \\times S^2$\nConsider the unforced ($f = 0, M = 0$) quadrotor-load system in taut mode Eqs. (4.16)–(4.21).\n\n\n1. Unit-norm invariance ($|q(t)| = 1$). From Eq. (4.18), $\\frac{d}{dt}(q \\cdot q) = 2 q \\cdot (\\omega \\times q) = 0$. Furthermore, from Eq. (4.19) with $f = 0$, $\\dot{\\omega} = 0$, so $\\frac{d}{dt}(\\omega \\cdot q) = \\omega \\cdot (\\omega \\times q) = 0$.\n2. Total mechanical energy conservation. Differentiating $E = T + U$ from Eqs. (4.11)–(4.12) along trajectories of Eqs. (4.16)–(4.21) with $f = 0, M = 0$ yields $\\dot{E} = 0$. Specifically, $v_Q = v_L - l(\\omega \\times q)$ satisfies $\\dot{v}_L + g e_3 = -\\frac{m_Q l}{m_Q + m_L}(\\omega \\cdot \\omega) q$ and $\\dot{v}_Q + g e_3 = \\frac{m_L l}{m_Q + m_L}(\\omega \\cdot \\omega) q$, so all internal tension work cancels identically.\nExample 4.2: Hover Equilibrium and Cable Tension\nAt hover ($\\dot{x}L = 0, \\dot{v}L = 0, \\omega = 0, \\Omega = 0$), Eq. (4.17) gives $$(m_Q + m_L) g e_3 = (q \\cdot f R e_3) q. \\tag{4.35}$$ With $q{\\text{eq}} = -e_3$ (load hanging directly below the quadrotor) and $R{\\text{eq}} = I$, we have $q_{\\text{eq}} \\cdot f R_{\\text{eq}} e_3 = -f$, yielding $f_{\\text{eq}} = (m_Q + m_L) g$ and cable tension $T_{\\text{eq}} = |m_L g e_3| = m_L g > 0$.\n\n\n________________\n\n\n4.6 Exercises\n1. Covariant Acceleration on $S^2$. Starting from the constrained Lagrangian $L(q, \\dot{q}) = \\frac{1}{2} m l^2 (\\dot{q} \\cdot \\dot{q}) - m g l (e_3 \\cdot q)$ with $q \\in S^2$, use the variation $\\delta q = \\xi \\times q$ ($\\xi \\cdot q = 0$) to derive $\\nabla_{\\dot{q}}\\dot{q} = \\ddot{q} + (\\dot{q}\\cdot\\dot{q})q = \\frac{g}{l}(q \\times (q \\times e_3))$.\n2. Degrees of Underactuation. Verify that a $p$-quadrotor system carrying a rigid-body payload via $p$ flexible cables of $n_i$ links each has $6 + 3p + 2\\sum_{i=1}^p n_i$ DOF and $6 - p + 2\\sum_{i=1}^p n_i$ degrees of underactuation (Kotaru, Wu, & Sreenath, arXiv:1711.04895).\n3. Lie-Group Numerical Integration. Implement a geometric RK4 integrator for Eqs. (4.16)–(4.21) that updates $R \\in SO(3)$ via $\\exp(\\Delta t,\\Omega^\\wedge)$ and $q \\in S^2$ via Rodrigues' rotation $\\exp(\\Delta t,\\omega^\\wedge)q$. Verify that $|q(t)| - 1$ remains at machine precision ($\\sim 10^{-15}$) and compare energy drift $\\Delta E / E_0$ against naive Euler integration.\n4. Modular Mass Matrix. Using Eq. (4.34) from arXiv:2504.16383, show how removing leg $k$ from a hexapod reduces to subtracting $\\sum_{j=1}^{n_k} M_{kj}(\\theta)$ and slicing the corresponding joint rows/columns without recomputing $\\partial M / \\partial \\theta_i$ for the remaining legs.\n\n```mermaid\nstateDiagram-v2\n  [*] --> TautMode: \"T > 0 on SE(3) x S^2\"\n  TautMode --> SlackMode: \"Guard S_z: T = 0\"\n  SlackMode --> TautMode: \"Guard S_n: ||x_Q - x_L|| = l\"\n```\n"}, {"id": "ch07", "part": "Part III: Geometric Control", "label": "Ch 7. Variation Linearization & LQR", "verified": true, "chapterFile": "ch07_variation_linearization_lqr.md", "notebook": "ch07_variation_lqr_sim.ipynb", "skill": "ch07-variation-lqr-design", "diagramKey": "variation_lqr_tube", "markdown": "﻿Chapter 7: Variation-Based Linearization, State-Constrained Controllability & LTV-LQR\nLinearizing nonlinear mechanical systems evolving on curved configuration manifolds—such as $SO(3)$, $S^2$, and their products $\\prod_i M_i$—using local coordinates (e.g., Euler angles or spherical coordinates) introduces representation singularities and cumbersome trigonometric expressions. Building on the coordinate-free variation framework of Wu & Sreenath (IEEE Access 2015) and its extension to multi-link cable-suspended aerial systems in Kotaru, Wu, & Sreenath (arXiv:1711.04895), this chapter develops symbolic variation-based linearization, establishes state-constrained controllability on the invariant nullspace of the tangent-bundle constraints, and synthesizes finite-horizon LTV-LQR controllers directly on the manifold.\n\n\n________________\n\n\n7.1 Notation and Lie-Algebra Preliminaries\nWe use the standard hat map $(\\cdot)^\\wedge : \\mathbb{R}^3 \\to \\mathfrak{so}(3)$ defined by\n\n\n$$ x^\\wedge y = x \\times y, \\qquad \\forall x, y \\in \\mathbb{R}^3, \\tag{7.1} $$\n\n\nand its inverse vee map $(\\cdot)^\\vee : \\mathfrak{so}(3) \\to \\mathbb{R}^3$ satisfying $(x^\\wedge)^\\vee = x$. For any $x, y \\in \\mathbb{R}^3$ and $R \\in SO(3)$, the hat map obeys $x^\\wedge y = -y^\\wedge x$, $(x^\\wedge)^T = -x^\\wedge$, and $(R x)^\\wedge = R x^\\wedge R^T$.\n\n\n________________\n\n\n7.2 Variation-Based Linearization on Product Manifolds $\\prod_i M_i$\nConsider a mechanical system evolving on a product manifold $\\mathcal{Q} = \\prod_{i=1}^k M_i$, where each factor $M_i \\in {\\mathbb{R}^3, S^2, SO(3)}$, with state $x(t) \\in T\\mathcal{Q}$ and control input $u(t) \\in \\mathbb{R}^m$. Given a smooth, dynamically feasible desired trajectory $(x_d(t), u_d(t))$, we perturb the trajectory via a smooth one-parameter family $(x_\\epsilon(t), u_\\epsilon(t))$ with $x_0(t) = x_d(t)$ and $u_0(t) = u_d(t)$, and define the variation $\\delta x = \\left.\\frac{d}{d\\epsilon}\\right|{\\epsilon=0} x\\epsilon$ and $\\delta u = \\left.\\frac{d}{d\\epsilon}\\right|{\\epsilon=0} u\\epsilon$.\n\n\nManifold Factor $M_i$\n\tConfiguration Variation\n\tVelocity Variation\n\tAlgebraic Constraint $C_i(x_d) s_i = 0$\n\t$\\mathbb{R}^3$\n\t$\\delta p = \\delta p \\in \\mathbb{R}^3$\n\t$\\delta v = \\delta \\dot{p} \\in \\mathbb{R}^3$\n\tNone ($C_i = 0$)\n\t$S^2$\n\t$\\delta q = \\xi \\times q_d = -q_d^\\wedge \\xi$\n\t$\\delta \\omega \\in \\mathbb{R}^3$\n\t$\\xi \\cdot q_d = 0$, $(\\xi \\times q_d) \\cdot \\omega_d + q_d \\cdot \\delta \\omega = 0$\n\t$SO(3)$\n\t$\\delta R = R_d \\eta^\\wedge$\n\t$\\delta \\Omega = \\Omega_d^\\wedge \\eta + \\dot{\\eta}$\n\tNone ($C_i = 0$)\n\t\n\nTable 7.1: Canonical tangent-space variations and algebraic constraints on elementary manifold factors $M_i \\in {\\mathbb{R}^3, S^2, SO(3)}$ (Wu & Sreenath, 2015).\n7.2.1 Variations on $\\mathbb{R}^3$\nFor $p \\in \\mathbb{R}^3$ with $\\dot{p} = v$, the perturbed state $p_\\epsilon = p_d + \\epsilon \\delta p$ yields linear kinematics without constraints:\n\n\n$$ \\delta \\dot{p} = \\delta v. \\tag{7.2} $$\n7.2.2 Variations on $SO(3)$\nFor $R \\in SO(3)$ with body-frame angular velocity $\\Omega \\in \\mathbb{R}^3$ ($\\dot{R} = R \\Omega^\\wedge$), the manifold-preserving perturbation is $R_\\epsilon(t) = R_d(t) \\exp(\\epsilon \\eta^\\wedge(t))$ with $\\eta(t) \\in \\mathbb{R}^3$, giving\n\n\n$$ \\delta R = R_d \\eta^\\wedge. \\tag{7.3} $$\n\n\nDifferentiating $\\delta R$ with respect to time and equating it to $\\delta(R \\Omega^\\wedge) = \\delta R,\\Omega_d^\\wedge + R_d,\\delta\\Omega^\\wedge$ yields $R_d(\\Omega_d^\\wedge \\eta^\\wedge + \\dot{\\eta}^\\wedge) = R_d(\\eta^\\wedge \\Omega_d^\\wedge + \\delta\\Omega^\\wedge)$. Using $[\\Omega_d^\\wedge, \\eta^\\wedge] = (\\Omega_d^\\wedge \\eta)^\\wedge$, we obtain\n\n\n$$ \\delta \\Omega = \\Omega_d^\\wedge \\eta + \\dot{\\eta} \\quad \\Longleftrightarrow \\quad \\dot{\\eta} = -\\Omega_d^\\wedge \\eta + \\delta \\Omega. \\tag{7.4} $$\n\n\nBecause $\\eta \\in \\mathbb{R}^3$ and $\\delta \\Omega \\in \\mathbb{R}^3$ are unconstrained, the variation state $s_{SO(3)} = [\\eta^T, \\delta \\Omega^T]^T \\in \\mathbb{R}^6$ requires no algebraic constraint.\n7.2.3 Variations on $S^2$\nFor $q \\in S^2 = {q \\in \\mathbb{R}^3 : q^T q = 1}$ with $\\dot{q} = \\omega \\times q$ and $q \\cdot \\omega = 0$, the perturbation $q_\\epsilon(t) = \\exp(\\epsilon \\xi^\\wedge(t)) q_d(t)$ with $\\xi \\in T_{q_d} S^2$ yields\n\n\n$$ \\delta q = \\xi \\times q_d = -q_d^\\wedge \\xi, \\qquad \\xi \\cdot q_d = 0. \\tag{7.5} $$\n\n\nTaking the variation of $q \\cdot \\omega = 0$ gives the velocity-level constraint\n\n\n$$ (\\xi \\times q_d) \\cdot \\omega_d + q_d \\cdot \\delta \\omega = 0 \\quad \\Longleftrightarrow \\quad (q_d^\\wedge \\omega_d)^T \\xi + q_d^T \\delta \\omega = 0. \\tag{7.6} $$\n\n\nEquating $\\frac{d}{dt}(\\delta q) = \\dot{\\xi} \\times q_d + \\xi \\times (\\omega_d \\times q_d)$ with $\\delta \\dot{q} = \\delta \\omega \\times q_d + \\omega_d \\times (\\xi \\times q_d)$ and applying the Jacobi identity gives $(\\dot{\\xi} - \\delta \\omega - \\omega_d \\times \\xi) \\times q_d = 0$. Enforcing $\\frac{d}{dt}(\\xi \\cdot q_d) = 0$ and Eq. (7.6) uniquely determines the normal component along $q_d$, yielding\n\n\n$$ \\dot{\\xi} = \\omega_d^\\wedge \\xi + (I_3 - q_d q_d^T) \\delta \\omega. \\tag{7.7} $$\n\n\nStacking $s_{S^2} = [\\xi^T, \\delta \\omega^T]^T \\in \\mathbb{R}^6$, the constraints Eqs. (7.5)–(7.6) take the compact form $C_{S^2}(t) s_{S^2} = 0$, where\n\n\n$$ C_{S^2}(t) = \\begin{bmatrix} q_d^T & 0_{1\\times 3} \\ (q_d^\\wedge \\omega_d)^T & q_d^T \\end{bmatrix} \\in \\mathbb{R}^{2 \\times 6}. \\tag{7.8} $$\n7.2.4 General Constrained LTV System on $\\prod_i M_i$\nAssembling the variation vectors $s_i$ of all manifold factors into $s \\in \\mathbb{R}^n$ and linearizing the Newton–Euler equations of motion about $(x_d(t), u_d(t))$ yields the symbolic constrained linear time-varying (LTV) system (Wu & Sreenath, 2015; Kotaru, Wu, & Sreenath, 2017):\n\n\n$$ \\dot{s} = A(x_d(t)) s + B(x_d(t)) \\delta u, \\tag{7.9a} $$ $$ C(x_d(t)) s = 0. \\tag{7.9b} $$\n\n\nHere $A(x_d(t)) \\in \\mathbb{R}^{n \\times n}$, $B(x_d(t)) \\in \\mathbb{R}^{n \\times m}$, and $C(x_d(t)) \\in \\mathbb{R}^{p \\times n}$ are expressed purely in terms of $(x_d(t), u_d(t))$ without local coordinate charts.\n\n\n________________\n\n\n7.3 State-Constrained Controllability\nBecause the variation state $s(t)$ is restricted to the time-varying nullspace $\\mathcal{N}(C(x_d(t))) = {s \\in \\mathbb{R}^n : C(x_d(t)) s = 0}$, classical unconstrained controllability criteria cannot be applied blindly without first verifying that the constraint manifold is invariant under the linearized flow.\n\n\nDef. 7.1 (State-Constrained Controllability). The constrained LTV system Eq. (7.9) on $[t_0, t_f]$ is state-constrained controllable if for every initial state $s_0 \\in \\mathcal{N}(C(x_d(t_0)))$ and every terminal state $s_f \\in \\mathcal{N}(C(x_d(t_f)))$, there exists a piecewise continuous control input $\\delta u : [t_0, t_f] \\to \\mathbb{R}^m$ such that the solution $s(t)$ of Eq. (7.9a) with $s(t_0) = s_0$ satisfies $s(t_f) = s_f$ and $C(x_d(t)) s(t) = 0$ for all $t \\in [t_0, t_f]$ (Wu & Sreenath, 2015).\n\n\nLemma 7.1 (Time-Invariance of $\\mathcal{N}(C)$). Suppose that for all $t \\in [t_0, t_f]$,\n\n\n$$ C(t) B(t) = 0 \\quad \\text{and} \\quad \\big(C(t) A(t) + \\dot{C}(t)\\big) s = 0 ;;; \\forall s \\in \\mathcal{N}(C(t)). \\tag{7.10} $$\n\n\nThen $\\frac{d}{dt}\\big(C(t) s(t)\\big) \\equiv 0$ along every trajectory of Eq. (7.9a). Consequently, if $s(t_0) \\in \\mathcal{N}(C(t_0))$, then $s(t) \\in \\mathcal{N}(C(t))$ for all $t \\ge t_0$, regardless of the control input $\\delta u(t)$.\n\n\nProof. Differentiating $C(t) s(t)$ along Eq. (7.9a) gives $\\frac{d}{dt}(C s) = (CA + \\dot{C}) s + CB,\\delta u$. Under Eq. (7.10), $\\frac{d}{dt}(Cs) = 0$ whenever $s \\in \\mathcal{N}(C)$, so $C(t)s(t) = C(t_0)s(t_0) = 0$ for all $t \\ge t_0$. $\\square$\n\n\nRemark 7.1. Lemma 7.1 is the cornerstone of constrained LTV control on manifolds: once $s(t_0) \\in \\mathcal{N}(C(t_0))$ is satisfied by construction of the variation map, the unconstrained dynamics Eq. (7.9a) automatically preserve Eq. (7.9b) for all $\\delta u(t)$.\n\n\nTo test controllability on $\\mathcal{N}(C(t))$, define the linear differential operator\n\n\n$$ s(B) = \\dot{B}(t) + A(t) B(t), \\qquad s^0(B) = B(t), \\qquad s^k(B) = s\\big(s^{k-1}(B)\\big). \\tag{7.11} $$\n\n\nProp. 7.1 (State-Constrained Controllability Criterion). Assume Lemma 7.1 holds and $A(t), B(t)$ are sufficiently smooth on $[t_0, t_f]$. If there exists $k \\ge 1$ such that the columns of the controllability matrix\n\n\n$$ \\mathcal{C}_k(t) = \\begin{bmatrix} B(t) & s(B)(t) & \\cdots & s^{k-1}(B)(t) \\end{bmatrix} \\tag{7.12} $$\n\n\nspan $\\mathcal{N}(C(t))$ (i.e., $\\operatorname{rank}(\\mathcal{C}_k(t)) = \\dim \\mathcal{N}(C(t)) = n - \\operatorname{rank}(C(t))$) for all $t \\in [t_0, t_f]$, then Eq. (7.9) is state-constrained controllable on $[t_0, t_f]$ (Wu & Sreenath, 2015).\n7.3.1 Application to the Spherical Pendulum on $S^2$\nConsider a spherical pendulum of mass $m$ and length $l$ with $q \\in S^2$ and $q \\cdot \\omega = 0$:\n\n\n$$ \\dot{q} = \\omega \\times q, \\qquad \\dot{\\omega} = \\frac{g}{l} e_3^\\wedge q + \\frac{1}{m l^2}(I_3 - q q^T) u. \\tag{7.13} $$\n\n\nLinearizing Eq. (7.13) about $(q_d(t), \\omega_d(t), u_d(t))$ using Eqs. (7.5)–(7.7) gives $s = [\\xi^T, \\delta \\omega^T]^T \\in \\mathbb{R}^6$ with $C(t) = C_{S^2}(t)$ from Eq. (7.8) and\n\n\n$$ A(t) = \\begin{bmatrix} \\omega_d^\\wedge & I_3 - q_d q_d^T \\ A_{21}(t) & 0_{3\\times 3} \\end{bmatrix}, \\qquad B(t) = \\begin{bmatrix} 0_{3\\times 3} \\ \\frac{1}{m l^2}(I_3 - q_d q_d^T) \\end{bmatrix}, \\tag{7.14} $$\n\n\nwhere $A_{21}(t) = -\\frac{g}{l} e_3^\\wedge q_d^\\wedge + \\frac{1}{ml^2}\\big((q_d^T u_d)I_3 + q_d u_d^T\\big) q_d^\\wedge$. Direct differentiation confirms $C B = 0$ and $(CA + \\dot{C}) s = 0$ for all $s \\in \\mathcal{N}(C)$ (verified numerically to $\\mathcal{O}(10^{-9})$ in ch07_variation_lqr_sim.ipynb). Moreover,\n\n\n$$ s(B) = \\dot{B} + A B = \\begin{bmatrix} \\frac{1}{m l^2}(I_3 - q_d q_d^T) \\ -\\frac{1}{m l^2}(\\dot{q}_d q_d^T + q_d \\dot{q}_d^T) \\end{bmatrix}. \\tag{7.15} $$\n\n\nSince $\\operatorname{rank}(I_3 - q_d q_d^T) = 2$ and the upper and lower $3 \\times 3$ blocks of $[B, s(B)]$ are block-triangular with respect to $T_{q_d}S^2$, $\\operatorname{rank}([B, s(B)]) = 4 = \\dim \\mathcal{N}(C(t))$ for every smooth trajectory $(q_d(t), \\omega_d(t))$. By Prop. 7.1, the spherical pendulum is state-constrained controllable along all feasible trajectories.\n7.3.2 Application to the 3D Pendulum on $SO(3)$\nConsider a 3D rigid-body pendulum with attitude $R \\in SO(3)$, body angular velocity $\\Omega \\in \\mathbb{R}^3$, inertia $J \\succ 0$, mass $m$, and center-of-mass offset $\\rho \\in \\mathbb{R}^3$:\n\n\n$$ \\dot{R} = R \\Omega^\\wedge, \\qquad J \\dot{\\Omega} + \\Omega \\times J \\Omega = -m g \\rho^\\wedge R^T e_3 + u. \\tag{7.16} $$\n\n\nApplying Eqs. (7.3)–(7.4) with $s = [\\eta^T, \\delta \\Omega^T]^T \\in \\mathbb{R}^6$ ($C(t) = 0$) yields\n\n\n$$ A(t) = \\begin{bmatrix} -\\Omega_d^\\wedge & I_3 \\ -m g J^{-1} \\rho^\\wedge (R_d^T e_3)^\\wedge & J^{-1}\\big((J\\Omega_d)^\\wedge - \\Omega_d^\\wedge J\\big) \\end{bmatrix}, \\qquad B(t) = \\begin{bmatrix} 0_{3\\times 3} \\ J^{-1} \\end{bmatrix}. \\tag{7.17} $$\n\n\nHere $s(B) = \\dot{B} + AB = \\begin{bmatrix} J^{-1} \\ J^{-1}((J\\Omega_d)^\\wedge - \\Omega_d^\\wedge J) J^{-1} \\end{bmatrix}$, so $\\operatorname{rank}([B, s(B)]) = 6 = \\dim(\\mathbb{R}^6)$ for all $t$, proving controllability along every trajectory on $SO(3)$.\n\n\n________________\n\n\n7.4 Finite-Horizon Riccati LTV-LQR Design\nBecause Lemma 7.1 guarantees that $\\mathcal{N}(C(t))$ is forward-invariant under Eq. (7.9a), we can synthesize a finite-horizon Linear Quadratic Regulator directly on the unconstrained pair $(A(t), B(t))$ (Kotaru, Wu, & Sreenath, 2017). Given symmetric weighting matrices $Q(t) \\succeq 0$, $P_f \\succeq 0$, and $R(t) \\succ 0$, we minimize\n\n\n$$ J = \\frac{1}{2} s(t_f)^T P_f s(t_f) + \\frac{1}{2} \\int_{t_0}^{t_f} \\Big(s(t)^T Q(t) s(t) + \\delta u(t)^T R(t) \\delta u(t)\\Big) dt. \\tag{7.18} $$\n\n\nThe optimal feedback gain is obtained by integrating the Continuous-Time Differential Riccati Equation (DRE) backward from $t_f$ to $t_0$:\n\n\n$$ -\\dot{P}(t) = A(t)^T P(t) + P(t) A(t) - P(t) B(t) R(t)^{-1} B(t)^T P(t) + Q(t), \\qquad P(t_f) = P_f, \\tag{7.19} $$\n\n\nyielding the LTV-LQR control law\n\n\n$$ K(t) = R(t)^{-1} B(t)^T P(t), \\qquad \\delta u(t) = -K(t) s(t), \\qquad u(t) = u_d(t) + \\delta u(t). \\tag{7.20} $$\n\n\nTo evaluate $s(t)$ from the nonlinear state $(x(t), x_d(t))$ at runtime:\n\n\n* On $SO(3)$: $\\eta = \\big(\\log(R_d^T R)\\big)^\\vee$ and $\\delta \\Omega = \\Omega - \\Omega_d$.\n* On $S^2$: $\\xi = \\frac{\\theta}{\\sin\\theta}(q_d \\times q)$ with $\\theta = \\arccos(q_d^T q)$, and $\\delta \\omega = \\omega - \\omega_d - \\big(q_d \\cdot (\\omega - \\omega_d) + (\\xi \\times q_d)\\cdot \\omega_d\\big) q_d$, which enforces $C_{S^2}(t) s(t) = 0$ to machine precision even at large initial errors.\n\n\n________________\n\n\n7.5 Worked Examples\nWorked Example 7.1: Spherical Pendulum Tracking from $50^\\circ$ Initial Error\nIn ch07_variation_lqr_sim.ipynb, we simulate Eq. (7.13) with $m = 1.0\\text{ kg}$, $l = 1.0\\text{ m}$, $g = 9.81\\text{ m/s}^2$ along a time-varying conical figure-8 trajectory on $S^2$ over $t \\in [0, 10]\\text{ s}$, using $Q = \\operatorname{diag}(40, 40, 40, 8, 8, 8)$, $R = 0.5 I_3$, and $P_f = \\operatorname{diag}(50, 50, 50, 10, 10, 10)$. Starting from a $50.00^\\circ$ attitude error and velocity perturbation $[0.8, -0.6, 0]^T\\text{ rad/s}$:\n\n\n* Constraint invariance holds with $\\max_t |\\frac{d}{dt}(C s)| = 1.104 \\times 10^{-9}$ and $\\max_t |C(t)s(t)| = 6.942 \\times 10^{-16}$.\n* Controllability rank is $\\min_t \\operatorname{rank}([B, s(B)]) = 4 = \\dim \\mathcal{N}(C)$.\n* Closed-loop tracking converges to $0.0000^\\circ$ attitude error and $1.853 \\times 10^{-9}\\text{ rad/s}$ velocity error (Fig. 7.1 in ch07_variation_lqr_sim.ipynb).\nWorked Example 7.2: 3D Pendulum Tracking on $SO(3)$ from $60^\\circ$ Initial Error\nFor the 3D pendulum Eq. (7.16) with $m = 1.5\\text{ kg}$, $J = \\operatorname{diag}(0.12, 0.15, 0.08)\\text{ kg}\\cdot\\text{m}^2$, and $\\rho = [0, 0, -0.5]^T\\text{ m}$ over $t \\in [0, 10]\\text{ s}$, using $Q = \\operatorname{diag}(50, 50, 50, 5, 5, 5)$ and $R = 0.2 I_3$:\n\n\n* Controllability rank is $\\min_t \\operatorname{rank}([B, s(B)]) = 6$.\n* Integrating the nonlinear dynamics with a geometric Lie-group Heun integrator preserves $\\max_t |\\det(R) - 1| = 6.883 \\times 10^{-15}$.\n* Starting from a $60.00^\\circ$ attitude error, the LTV-LQR controller stabilizes the trajectory to $0.0004^\\circ$ attitude error and $3.972 \\times 10^{-4}\\text{ rad/s}$ velocity error (Fig. 7.2 in ch07_variation_lqr_sim.ipynb).\n\n\n________________\n\n\n7.6 Exercises\n1. Product Manifold Linearization ($SE(3) \\times S^2$): Derive $(A(t), B(t), C(t))$ for a rigid-body quadrotor ($SE(3) = \\mathbb{R}^3 \\times SO(3)$) carrying a point-mass payload via a single rigid massless link ($S^2$), following Kotaru, Wu, & Sreenath (2017). Verify that $C(t) \\in \\mathbb{R}^{2 \\times 16}$ and $\\dim \\mathcal{N}(C) = 14$.\n2. Invariance Proof: Prove analytically that for the spherical pendulum Eq. (7.13)–(7.14), the second row of $(CA + \\dot{C}) s$ vanishes identically for all $s \\in \\mathcal{N}(C)$ even when $u_d(t)$ has a nonzero normal component $q_d^T u_d \\neq 0$.\n3. Controllability with Restricted Input: Suppose the spherical pendulum is actuated only by a horizontal force $f \\in \\mathbb{R}^2$ in the $e_1$–$e_2$ plane so that $u = q \\times [f_1, f_2, 0]^T$. When does $\\operatorname{rank}([B, s(B)])$ drop below $4$?\n4. Riccati Projection Comparison: In ch07_variation_lqr_sim.ipynb, compare the closed-loop trajectory when $P(t)$ is integrated directly in $\\mathbb{R}^{6\\times 6}$ versus projecting $P(t)$ onto $\\mathcal{N}(C(t))$ via $N(t)^T P(t) N(t)$. Explain why both yield identical feedback $\\delta u = -K(t) s(t)$ when $s(t) \\in \\mathcal{N}(C(t))$.\n\n```mermaid\nflowchart LR\n  NOM[\"Nominal x_d(t) on M\"] -->|\"Symbolic Variations\"| LTV[\"LTV (A(t), B(t)) on T_{x_d}M\"]\n  LTV -->|\"Projected Riccati P(t)\"| LQR[\"Feedback du = -K(t) dx\"]\n```\n"}];
